WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.485 align:middle line:90%


00:00:02.485 --> 00:00:03.479 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:03.479 --> 00:00:04.970 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:05.964 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.964 --> 00:00:13.412 align:middle line:90%


00:00:13.412 --> 00:00:14.120 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: OK.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:15.060 align:middle line:90%
So what is GKR?

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:19.092 align:middle line:90%


00:00:19.092 --> 00:00:20.050 align:middle line:90%
Can you guys read this?

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:20.530 align:middle line:90%
Is this too big?

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:21.130 align:middle line:90%
Too small?

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:21.896 align:middle line:90%
It's fine?

00:00:21.896 --> 00:00:22.729 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It's fine.

00:00:22.729 --> 00:00:27.512 align:middle line:90%


00:00:27.512 --> 00:00:28.220 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: OK.

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:31.020 align:middle line:84%
So the idea is
given a circuit C,

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:34.060 align:middle line:84%
we're going to-- instead of hash
computing circuit C by yourself,

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:35.630 align:middle line:84%
you can delegate
it to a prover who

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:37.380 align:middle line:84%
would do the computation
for you and prove

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.160 align:middle line:84%
the computation is correct
in an interactive protocol.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.320 align:middle line:84%
And the main idea of
how you can do it--

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:52.200 align:middle line:84%
So let's say this is the depth
D and size S. What you're

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.030 align:middle line:84%
going to do is
you're going to take

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:57.210 align:middle line:84%
every single layer
of the circuit,

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.560 align:middle line:84%
extend it to a
low-degree extension.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:03.100 align:middle line:90%


00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:05.923 align:middle line:90%
So is this going to be the hat.

00:01:05.923 --> 00:01:07.840 align:middle line:84%
So you're basically going
to take all the wire

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.530 align:middle line:84%
values on each layer, find a
low-degree extension of it,

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:14.170 align:middle line:84%
and then you're going to have
some low-degree extension

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:15.370 align:middle line:90%
of each layer.

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:20.420 align:middle line:84%
The top layer is going to be the
V1, then V1 layer, and so on.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:23.100 align:middle line:84%
The final layer
is going to be VD.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.510 align:middle line:84%
And the way GKR works is
essentially as follows.

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:27.880 align:middle line:84%
You're going to reduce some
claims about the top layer--

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:28.680 align:middle line:90%
the upper layer--

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:30.360 align:middle line:90%
to claims about layers below it.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:31.230 align:middle line:84%
And you're going
to keep doing this

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:33.230 align:middle line:84%
until you reach the bottom
layer, at which point

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:35.190 align:middle line:84%
the verifier can
check themselves.

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:39.600 align:middle line:84%
So at the beginning, you might
have that the output gate--

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.850 align:middle line:90%
you want this to be equal to 0.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:45.390 align:middle line:84%
You're going to reduce this,
via sum check protocol,

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:46.990 align:middle line:90%
to two claims.

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:53.700 align:middle line:84%
So let's call this V11 equals,
maybe, the value of V11.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.120 align:middle line:84%
And also, the claim that
the low-degree extension

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.160 align:middle line:90%
on the second point--

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:02.618 align:middle line:84%
so that's also equal to
the different value of you

00:02:02.618 --> 00:02:03.160 align:middle line:90%
want it here.

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:06.540 align:middle line:84%
And this is [INAUDIBLE]
assumption protocol.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:07.780 align:middle line:90%
And then you can do it again.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:10.960 align:middle line:84%
So last time, [INAUDIBLE]
two-to-two fashion.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:13.210 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to reduce the
two claims to two claims,

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:14.222 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

00:02:14.222 --> 00:02:23.722 align:middle line:90%


00:02:23.722 --> 00:02:24.930 align:middle line:90%
I'm just going to keep going.

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:26.972 align:middle line:84%
At the end, you're going
to reduce the assumption

00:02:26.972 --> 00:02:29.160 align:middle line:84%
to the claim about
the output layer.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:30.703 align:middle line:90%
So maybe ZD1.

00:02:30.703 --> 00:02:40.950 align:middle line:90%


00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:43.170 align:middle line:84%
And at this point in
time, this is just

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:45.090 align:middle line:84%
a low-degree extension
of all the input

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:47.670 align:middle line:90%
gates, which is size n.

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:49.620 align:middle line:84%
The verifier knows what
all these inputs are,

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:51.453 align:middle line:84%
so they can compute the
low-degree extension

00:02:51.453 --> 00:02:52.170 align:middle line:90%
by themselves.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:53.940 align:middle line:84%
And so all together,
this is going

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:59.850 align:middle line:84%
to take a prover time poly (S)
to prove the computation's done

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:00.990 align:middle line:90%
correctly to the verifier.

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:05.610 align:middle line:84%
And the verifier
will run, in time--

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:11.190 align:middle line:84%
let's see it's going to
be D times polylog S,

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.025 align:middle line:84%
plus n times maybe
polylog S, also.

00:03:13.025 --> 00:03:15.965 align:middle line:90%


00:03:15.965 --> 00:03:17.340 align:middle line:84%
So this is what
we did last time.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:18.620 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:22.460 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Can you go over how
using the same randomness help

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:23.720 align:middle line:90%
you get the thing--

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:25.677 align:middle line:90%
you just do all the way down?

00:03:25.677 --> 00:03:26.510 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Right.

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:28.310 align:middle line:84%
So the idea is,
instead of having

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:30.050 align:middle line:84%
to run a different
subject protocol

00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:32.690 align:middle line:84%
with independent randomness for
each of these two claims, what

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:35.600 align:middle line:84%
you can do is use the same
randomness for both claims

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:36.800 align:middle line:90%
simultaneously.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:38.867 align:middle line:84%
And turns out that
the gates you have

00:03:38.867 --> 00:03:41.450 align:middle line:84%
to check at the very bottom, at
the end of sum check protocol,

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:43.895 align:middle line:84%
will be based on the
random method that

00:03:43.895 --> 00:03:46.080 align:middle line:84%
was used during from the
verifier's point of view.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:47.210 align:middle line:84%
So as long as the
same randomness

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:48.920 align:middle line:84%
using both of these
things, then you'll

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:51.777 align:middle line:84%
have to use the same two final
checks at the very bottom

00:03:51.777 --> 00:03:52.485 align:middle line:90%
of the sum check.

00:03:52.485 --> 00:03:55.250 align:middle line:90%


00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:56.060 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:59.030 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:03:59.030 --> 00:03:59.630 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:02.462 align:middle line:84%
And maybe I should
also just tell you--

00:04:02.462 --> 00:04:05.510 align:middle line:84%
yeah, I also wanted to
write out sum check equation

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:07.220 align:middle line:84%
just so we have it
for later because it's

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:09.298 align:middle line:90%
going to be important.

00:04:09.298 --> 00:04:11.340 align:middle line:84%
Used up an entire board
range is kind of awkward.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:14.330 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll put
it somewhere else.

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:15.237 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:04:15.237 --> 00:04:16.420 align:middle line:90%
Oh, it can move there.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:16.920 align:middle line:90%
Maybe?

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:17.560 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:20.060 align:middle line:84%
I guess-- I told the camera man
we wouldn't need that board.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:22.040 align:middle line:84%
But I'll just put it
here because it shouldn't

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:23.720 align:middle line:90%
be important, I guess.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:26.100 align:middle line:84%
Or it would be important for
us, but we did last time.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:27.350 align:middle line:90%
So hopefully, it won't be new.

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:31.626 align:middle line:90%


00:04:31.626 --> 00:04:34.970 align:middle line:84%
So you're checking an
element of the ith layer.

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:37.570 align:middle line:84%
You want to reduce it to 2 and
things in the previous layer.

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:41.590 align:middle line:84%
And so what you can do is you
can take an output gate and two

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:46.430 align:middle line:84%
input gates, and it's going
to be this giant thing.

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:57.440 align:middle line:84%
So whether or not it
was at gate times--

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:01.970 align:middle line:84%
times this one-- times--
whether-- times the sum of it.

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:28.458 align:middle line:90%


00:05:28.458 --> 00:05:30.500 align:middle line:84%
And then you need to take
a low-degree extension.

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:34.365 align:middle line:90%


00:05:34.365 --> 00:05:35.680 align:middle line:90%
And so that's the equation.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:38.263 align:middle line:84%
What's going to happen is you're
going to essentially do a sum

00:05:38.263 --> 00:05:41.525 align:middle line:84%
check with this entire thing
here as a polynomial in your sum

00:05:41.525 --> 00:05:42.610 align:middle line:90%
check.

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:44.123 align:middle line:84%
So we'll come back to this
later and we'll just leave it up

00:05:44.123 --> 00:05:44.270 align:middle line:90%
for now.

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:44.342 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Sorry.

00:05:44.342 --> 00:05:45.440 align:middle line:90%
What's the Q2?

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:47.603 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, it's
a polynomial of degree--

00:05:47.603 --> 00:05:50.200 align:middle line:90%


00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:52.450 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to say it's,
like, the size of H,

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:53.810 align:middle line:90%
maybe, in each variable.

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:57.550 align:middle line:84%
So individual degree
size h minus 1.

00:05:57.550 --> 00:05:59.880 align:middle line:90%
Or just take it over--

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:03.340 align:middle line:84%
to the M. And it's
going to basically test

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:04.850 align:middle line:90%
the quality of z and p.

00:06:04.850 --> 00:06:08.440 align:middle line:84%
So if z-- if p is
in this hypercube,

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:10.720 align:middle line:84%
then it should be 1, if and
only if p is equal to z,

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:12.470 align:middle line:84%
and then the low-degree
extension of that.

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:18.930 align:middle line:90%


00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:21.010 align:middle line:84%
OK, so today we're going
to ask some questions.

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:23.070 align:middle line:90%
So we knew how to do this.

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:24.940 align:middle line:84%
So last time, I
showed how to do this,

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:29.100 align:middle line:84%
as long as we had Oracle access
to the add and mult gates.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:32.130 align:middle line:84%
So the goal of the first
part of today's class

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:33.700 align:middle line:90%
is to remove this Oracle access.

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:35.997 align:middle line:84%
We're going to show how
to get these things.

00:06:35.997 --> 00:06:38.080 align:middle line:84%
Actually, we have a little
bit more in this board.

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:43.860 align:middle line:84%
So I guess-- so question
one is how to get

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:45.910 align:middle line:90%
these low-degree extensions.

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:51.365 align:middle line:90%


00:06:51.365 --> 00:06:52.490 align:middle line:90%
Maybe you guys can tell me.

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:53.240 align:middle line:90%
Do you have any ideas?

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:54.115 align:middle line:90%
How can we get these?

00:06:54.115 --> 00:07:00.752 align:middle line:90%


00:07:00.752 --> 00:07:02.710 align:middle line:84%
So I feel like Arnan is
bouncing, which clearly

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:03.960 align:middle line:90%
means he has something to say.

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:04.664 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Oh.

00:07:04.664 --> 00:07:06.193 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:06.193 --> 00:07:08.020 align:middle line:84%
Could you arithmetize
the circuit's

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:10.162 align:middle line:84%
[INAUDIBLE] succinct
description?

00:07:10.162 --> 00:07:11.120 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: OK, good.

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:13.440 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'm going to say
something like maybe you

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:15.190 align:middle line:84%
can compute these
things yourself somehow,

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:18.010 align:middle line:90%
if you can arithmetize it.

00:07:18.010 --> 00:07:20.200 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to see
how do this in a sec.

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:22.510 align:middle line:84%
Another way to do it is we
already know how to do GKR.

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:25.820 align:middle line:84%
Like, we already know how
to delegate a circuit.

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:28.780 align:middle line:84%
So let's redelegate
the add and mult gates.

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:37.410 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
redelegate add and mult.

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:41.362 align:middle line:84%
And this is, indeed, what they
do in the original GKR paper.

00:07:41.362 --> 00:07:42.820 align:middle line:84%
But we're not going
to do it today.

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:44.470 align:middle line:84%
So this is another
thing you could do.

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:48.100 align:middle line:84%
But today, we're going to
show how to compute them.

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:50.350 align:middle line:84%
Actually, this is a bit
weird because we're actually

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:53.017 align:middle line:84%
going to compute the low-degree
extensions of these add and mult

00:07:53.017 --> 00:07:53.620 align:middle line:90%
circuits.

00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:55.480 align:middle line:84%
We're actually going
to compute a different,

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:58.570 align:middle line:84%
somewhat low-degree
polynomial on these--

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:01.360 align:middle line:84%
that share the same
values on the hypercube.

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:07.120 align:middle line:84%
So it's actually not going
to be the canonical LDE.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:10.040 align:middle line:90%


00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:12.010 align:middle line:84%
I think last time,
Leo had said something

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:14.957 align:middle line:84%
about-- can you use a different
low-degree polynomial?

00:08:14.957 --> 00:08:16.540 align:middle line:84%
And the answer here
is, yes, and we're

00:08:16.540 --> 00:08:18.165 align:middle line:84%
going to see application
of that today.

00:08:18.165 --> 00:08:21.100 align:middle line:90%


00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:21.770 align:middle line:90%
OK, cool.

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:23.090 align:middle line:90%
So the idea is--

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:25.160 align:middle line:90%
so if you want to compute them--

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:27.890 align:middle line:84%
so you can't compute the actual
canonical low-degree extension.

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:29.110 align:middle line:90%
So what can you do?

00:08:29.110 --> 00:08:34.632 align:middle line:84%
Well-- and the
reason is basically

00:08:34.632 --> 00:08:36.590 align:middle line:84%
because if you compute
the add and mult gates--

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:38.507 align:middle line:84%
because the circuit is
too complicated for you

00:08:38.507 --> 00:08:40.730 align:middle line:90%
to compute yourself.

00:08:40.730 --> 00:08:42.480 align:middle line:90%
So maybe here's an idea.

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:44.479 align:middle line:90%
What if, instead of computing--

00:08:44.479 --> 00:08:46.170 align:middle line:84%
doing GKR in the
original circuit,

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:47.600 align:middle line:84%
we do it on a much
simpler circuit

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:49.100 align:middle line:84%
that computes the
same functionality

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:51.017 align:middle line:84%
as a circuit you're
trying to-- that you want?

00:08:51.017 --> 00:09:00.850 align:middle line:90%


00:09:00.850 --> 00:09:11.030 align:middle line:84%
So idea-- replace C with a
simpler to describe circuit.

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:16.340 align:middle line:90%


00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:18.090 align:middle line:84%
And I guess by
simpler to describe,

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:21.000 align:middle line:84%
I just mean that add and mult
should be easily computable.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:33.440 align:middle line:90%


00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:35.373 align:middle line:84%
And so let's use the
universal circuit.

00:09:35.373 --> 00:09:37.540 align:middle line:84%
So universal circuit is
going to be a circuit that's

00:09:37.540 --> 00:09:39.498 align:middle line:84%
going to take as inputs
the circuit that you're

00:09:39.498 --> 00:09:41.960 align:middle line:84%
trying to compute,
along with the input.

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:43.060 align:middle line:90%
And it's going to output--

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:45.310 align:middle line:84%
it's going to simulate the
computation of your circuit

00:09:45.310 --> 00:09:50.532 align:middle line:84%
C on the input x and
then compute C of x.

00:09:50.532 --> 00:09:52.990 align:middle line:84%
And the claim is going to be
that this universal circuit is

00:09:52.990 --> 00:09:54.865 align:middle line:84%
going to have a simpler
description, at least

00:09:54.865 --> 00:09:57.572 align:middle line:84%
for the add and mult gates,
than the original circuit

00:09:57.572 --> 00:09:59.030 align:middle line:84%
because it's going
to take us input

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:00.300 align:middle line:84%
like the description
of the circuit.

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:01.770 align:middle line:84%
And then it's going to
simulate description,

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:04.160 align:middle line:84%
but all these simulation is
easy to describe because it's

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:05.515 align:middle line:90%
very general and very easy.

00:10:05.515 --> 00:10:08.990 align:middle line:90%


00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:10.585 align:middle line:84%
So maybe let's see
how this will work.

00:10:10.585 --> 00:10:13.130 align:middle line:90%


00:10:13.130 --> 00:10:14.970 align:middle line:84%
So you have this
universal circuit here.

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:18.285 align:middle line:90%
It's going to look like this.

00:10:18.285 --> 00:10:20.910 align:middle line:84%
And it's going to take as input
a description of the circuit C,

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:23.940 align:middle line:90%
along with your input x.

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:25.650 align:middle line:90%
And then it's going to run--

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:29.945 align:middle line:84%
the hope is that this is
going to be D times poly log--

00:10:29.945 --> 00:10:32.837 align:middle line:90%


00:10:32.837 --> 00:10:33.670 align:middle line:90%
maybe it should be--

00:10:33.670 --> 00:10:34.370 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:36.015 align:middle line:90%
We'll talk about this later.

00:10:36.015 --> 00:10:37.390 align:middle line:84%
So this should
be, hopefully, not

00:10:37.390 --> 00:10:39.640 align:middle line:84%
so much bigger than the
circuit C because you're going

00:10:39.640 --> 00:10:41.020 align:middle line:90%
to want to do it efficiently.

00:10:41.020 --> 00:10:43.330 align:middle line:84%
What is this
description C over here?

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:44.960 align:middle line:90%
This is-- yeah, maybe I should--

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:45.460 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:50.810 align:middle line:90%


00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:52.540 align:middle line:90%
So what does the circuit C?

00:10:52.540 --> 00:10:55.390 align:middle line:84%
What should input
to this be like?

00:10:55.390 --> 00:10:58.575 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's basically going to be
a function that will tell you--

00:10:58.575 --> 00:11:01.570 align:middle line:90%


00:11:01.570 --> 00:11:03.070 align:middle line:84%
yeah, so I guess
it would tell you--

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:06.010 align:middle line:84%
for every single-- maybe
this should be an add 1

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:08.590 align:middle line:90%
and then mult 1.

00:11:08.590 --> 00:11:10.570 align:middle line:84%
So for both add and
mult, it'll tell if,

00:11:10.570 --> 00:11:13.125 align:middle line:84%
at a single location in
the original circuit, C--

00:11:13.125 --> 00:11:16.516 align:middle line:90%


00:11:16.516 --> 00:11:18.730 align:middle line:84%
if there is a gate
of that form or not.

00:11:18.730 --> 00:11:20.810 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to be a
pretty large description.

00:11:20.810 --> 00:11:22.230 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be
size 2 to the s.

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:25.889 align:middle line:90%


00:11:25.889 --> 00:11:26.930 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:30.520 align:middle line:90%


00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:31.600 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:33.350 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So the circuit
[INAUDIBLE] size.

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:35.660 align:middle line:84%
We're going to give a
description of size 2 to the s.

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:36.813 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yes.

00:11:36.813 --> 00:11:37.730 align:middle line:90%
We'll start with that.

00:11:37.730 --> 00:11:40.380 align:middle line:84%
And as Ted is alluding to-- so
would not be so good for us,

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:41.670 align:middle line:84%
but we'll discuss how
to do in a little bit.

00:11:41.670 --> 00:11:42.160 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: S this is just--

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:42.952 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

00:11:42.952 --> 00:11:45.978 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Just-- literally,
just two-bit strings.

00:11:45.978 --> 00:11:47.270 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:11:47.270 --> 00:11:48.120 align:middle line:90%
It's supposed to be really long.

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:50.328 align:middle line:84%
It's going to tell you what
every gate of the circuit

00:11:50.328 --> 00:11:52.560 align:middle line:90%
looks like.

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:55.090 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Didn't
pw1 and w2 range

00:11:55.090 --> 00:11:58.900 align:middle line:84%
over the horizontal
distance of the circuit,

00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:00.040 align:middle line:90%
not the vertical distance?

00:12:00.040 --> 00:12:02.770 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, OK.

00:12:02.770 --> 00:12:04.570 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we can have
pw1 and w2 range

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:06.290 align:middle line:84%
over all the possible
gates or something.

00:12:06.290 --> 00:12:07.720 align:middle line:84%
So we can change the
notation a little bit.

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:08.235 align:middle line:90%
That's OK.

00:12:08.235 --> 00:12:12.870 align:middle line:90%


00:12:12.870 --> 00:12:15.078 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:12:15.078 --> 00:12:15.745 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Sorry.

00:12:15.745 --> 00:12:16.010 align:middle line:90%
Last question.

00:12:16.010 --> 00:12:16.650 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

00:12:16.650 --> 00:12:17.950 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I guess this
is a very small detail,

00:12:17.950 --> 00:12:20.070 align:middle line:84%
but how do you handle
knots at the input layer?

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:21.750 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: So what you can
do-- this is also, actually,

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:23.670 align:middle line:84%
a huge issue with the GKR
protocol, because you only

00:12:23.670 --> 00:12:24.670 align:middle line:90%
have add and mult gates.

00:12:24.670 --> 00:12:28.110 align:middle line:84%
But you can do is you can have
, and then also the flip of x

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:29.162 align:middle line:90%
or the not of x.

00:12:29.162 --> 00:12:30.870 align:middle line:84%
And then now you can
simulate everything.

00:12:30.870 --> 00:12:31.412 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:12:31.412 --> 00:12:34.160 align:middle line:90%


00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:36.020 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: OK.

00:12:36.020 --> 00:12:38.187 align:middle line:84%
So my claim is basically
that this universal circuit

00:12:38.187 --> 00:12:39.812 align:middle line:84%
is going to be much
simpler to describe

00:12:39.812 --> 00:12:41.540 align:middle line:84%
than the original
circuit, C. And maybe

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:44.780 align:middle line:84%
let's just see why in,
hopefully, a not too in detail

00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:46.770 align:middle line:84%
view, but hopefully,
we can get an idea.

00:12:46.770 --> 00:12:48.183 align:middle line:90%
So the idea is--

00:12:48.183 --> 00:12:49.975 align:middle line:84%
let's say this is your
circuit C over here.

00:12:49.975 --> 00:12:55.297 align:middle line:90%


00:12:55.297 --> 00:12:57.630 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be a bunch of
layers with a bunch of gates

00:12:57.630 --> 00:12:59.047 align:middle line:84%
in each of the
layers, and they're

00:12:59.047 --> 00:13:01.120 align:middle line:84%
going to each have
some functionality.

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:04.475 align:middle line:90%


00:13:04.475 --> 00:13:07.400 align:middle line:84%
And so what the universal
circuit is basically going

00:13:07.400 --> 00:13:11.210 align:middle line:90%
to do is it's going to say--

00:13:11.210 --> 00:13:14.810 align:middle line:84%
I want you to compute this value
of this circuit C gate here.

00:13:14.810 --> 00:13:16.760 align:middle line:84%
Let me look at all
the previous ones,

00:13:16.760 --> 00:13:19.310 align:middle line:84%
because you don't know
which one is a tattoo.

00:13:19.310 --> 00:13:22.250 align:middle line:84%
So you're going to look at
all the possible values,

00:13:22.250 --> 00:13:24.740 align:middle line:84%
and you're going to, then,
see what their circuit C says

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:27.560 align:middle line:84%
and then compute the appropriate
function of the previous layer

00:13:27.560 --> 00:13:29.300 align:middle line:84%
based on what the
circuit C says.

00:13:29.300 --> 00:13:32.610 align:middle line:90%
So in particular-- yeah.

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:34.680 align:middle line:84%
So the particular
in the ith layer--

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:36.740 align:middle line:90%
so the value of the circuit--

00:13:36.740 --> 00:13:40.370 align:middle line:84%
of this thing in the ith
layer-- is going to be--

00:13:40.370 --> 00:13:44.365 align:middle line:84%
so maybe this is a layer,
and this is a gate.

00:13:44.365 --> 00:13:47.940 align:middle line:90%


00:13:47.940 --> 00:13:50.820 align:middle line:84%
And it's going to be a
sum of possible gates

00:13:50.820 --> 00:13:53.800 align:middle line:84%
in the previous layer
of the following thing.

00:13:53.800 --> 00:13:55.220 align:middle line:90%
So it's going to be whether C--

00:13:55.220 --> 00:14:01.940 align:middle line:90%


00:14:01.940 --> 00:14:03.890 align:middle line:84%
so it's going to be
whether C has an add gate

00:14:03.890 --> 00:14:05.910 align:middle line:84%
corresponding to the three
gates you're picking.

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:10.160 align:middle line:84%
So this is J, And then
maybe K and K prime,

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:17.250 align:middle line:90%
and then times the sum of them.

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:19.580 align:middle line:84%
So it's a similar thing
as in the GKR thing.

00:14:19.580 --> 00:14:23.940 align:middle line:84%
We're going to have an
add and a mult condition.

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:27.448 align:middle line:84%
So if it's an add gate, you're
going to compute the sum.

00:14:27.448 --> 00:14:28.990 align:middle line:84%
And if it's a mult
gate, you're going

00:14:28.990 --> 00:14:43.700 align:middle line:90%
to compute the mult. Is that OK?

00:14:43.700 --> 00:14:46.802 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What is that inside
the parentheses on the add gate.

00:14:46.802 --> 00:14:47.760 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, OK.

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:51.290 align:middle line:84%
So this is going to be
whether the circuit is

00:14:51.290 --> 00:14:53.550 align:middle line:84%
an add gate at these
particular three gates--

00:14:53.550 --> 00:14:55.710 align:middle line:90%
J, K and K prime.

00:14:55.710 --> 00:14:59.970 align:middle line:84%
And this is going to be
the values, V I plus 1K.

00:14:59.970 --> 00:15:01.260 align:middle line:90%
So it's what this value is.

00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:01.680 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:02.250 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah,
sorry So maybe this

00:15:02.250 --> 00:15:03.170 align:middle line:90%
is a little bit small.

00:15:03.170 --> 00:15:06.010 align:middle line:90%


00:15:06.010 --> 00:15:06.510 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:15:06.510 --> 00:15:08.343 align:middle line:84%
I don't really know how
to make this bigger.

00:15:08.343 --> 00:15:13.520 align:middle line:90%


00:15:13.520 --> 00:15:14.364 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:15:14.364 --> 00:15:15.947 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The subscript
on the C add--

00:15:15.947 --> 00:15:17.570 align:middle line:84%
I actually think of it
as an input [? text. ?]

00:15:17.570 --> 00:15:18.737 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, exactly.

00:15:18.737 --> 00:15:21.080 align:middle line:84%
So these are both going to
be given from the description

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:24.208 align:middle line:84%
of the circuit C, which--
this will also have to take--

00:15:24.208 --> 00:15:26.000 align:middle line:84%
the description of
circuit C, and it's also

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:27.320 align:middle line:90%
going to read into here.

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:28.190 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And this
is the same thing

00:15:28.190 --> 00:15:29.900 align:middle line:84%
that's written on the
board [INAUDIBLE].

00:15:29.900 --> 00:15:30.960 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, it's
essentially the same thing.

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:32.793 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's just not
[INAUDIBLE] section.

00:15:32.793 --> 00:15:34.964 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:15:34.964 --> 00:15:38.730 align:middle line:90%


00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:39.953 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

00:15:39.953 --> 00:15:40.620 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Sorry.

00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:40.980 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: No.

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:42.480 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I'm still
hung up on this.

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:46.350 align:middle line:84%
C add is defined over three
inputs, all of which range

00:15:46.350 --> 00:15:48.598 align:middle line:90%
from 1 to [? test, ?] right?

00:15:48.598 --> 00:15:49.390 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Sure.

00:15:49.390 --> 00:15:49.830 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:15:49.830 --> 00:15:50.170 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:15:50.170 --> 00:15:51.520 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Why is it a size 3?

00:15:51.520 --> 00:15:53.800 align:middle line:84%
So it's essentially the same
[INAUDIBLE] size like s cubed.

00:15:53.800 --> 00:15:54.100 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Good.

00:15:54.100 --> 00:15:54.600 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:55.120 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:15:55.120 --> 00:15:57.310 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to have to
deal with that in a bit.

00:15:57.310 --> 00:15:59.710 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, instead of saying
that the circuit C here is--

00:15:59.710 --> 00:16:01.750 align:middle line:84%
the description of
circuit C is too big--

00:16:01.750 --> 00:16:03.760 align:middle line:84%
we're going to have to
do something for that.

00:16:03.760 --> 00:16:04.385 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Wait.

00:16:04.385 --> 00:16:06.460 align:middle line:90%
So why is it 2 the s, though?

00:16:06.460 --> 00:16:07.780 align:middle line:90%
Is it because we have--

00:16:07.780 --> 00:16:08.030 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Sorry.

00:16:08.030 --> 00:16:08.530 align:middle line:90%
It's just s.

00:16:08.530 --> 00:16:09.050 align:middle line:90%
It's just S.

00:16:09.050 --> 00:16:09.300 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:09.590 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: You're right.

00:16:09.590 --> 00:16:10.132 align:middle line:90%
You're right.

00:16:10.132 --> 00:16:12.115 align:middle line:90%
It's just S.

00:16:12.115 --> 00:16:12.740 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Wait.

00:16:12.740 --> 00:16:14.310 align:middle line:90%
Wasn't it S-cubed?

00:16:14.310 --> 00:16:15.486 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: S-cubed?

00:16:15.486 --> 00:16:16.748 align:middle line:90%
Oh, yes.

00:16:16.748 --> 00:16:20.164 align:middle line:90%
