00:16:20.164 --> 00:16:21.140 align:middle line:90%


00:16:21.140 --> 00:16:22.960 align:middle line:90%
Does that makes sense?

00:16:22.960 --> 00:16:23.460 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:16:23.460 --> 00:16:25.590 align:middle line:84%
I tried to explain that
[INAUDIBLE] really badly.

00:16:25.590 --> 00:16:28.920 align:middle line:84%
But you're basically trying to
simulate the computation of C.

00:16:28.920 --> 00:16:33.660 align:middle line:84%
And to do that you're going
to introduce a new circuit--

00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:36.750 align:middle line:84%
like, a new small circuit
for every single gate

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:39.335 align:middle line:84%
in your universal circuit that
will look at the previous layer

00:16:39.335 --> 00:16:41.460 align:middle line:84%
and then compute appropriate
functionality based on

00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:44.860 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE] is supposed to say.

00:16:44.860 --> 00:16:46.052 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:16:46.052 --> 00:16:46.760 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Rachel?

00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:47.552 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

00:16:47.552 --> 00:16:49.500 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What is the
p [INAUDIBLE] on this?

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:50.710 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, OK.

00:16:50.710 --> 00:16:52.468 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: On the
off-camera board.

00:16:52.468 --> 00:16:53.510 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, yeah.

00:16:53.510 --> 00:16:56.560 align:middle line:84%
So-- yeah-- so these are
all going to be H to the m.

00:16:56.560 --> 00:16:59.840 align:middle line:84%
H to the m here is going to
be the size of, like, a layer.

00:16:59.840 --> 00:17:00.730 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK, OK.

00:17:00.730 --> 00:17:02.188 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: So
it's going to be--

00:17:02.188 --> 00:17:04.220 align:middle line:84%
so p you can think of as
being in the ith layer.

00:17:04.220 --> 00:17:07.603 align:middle line:84%
And then omega 1 and omega 2
are in the i plus 1th layer.

00:17:07.603 --> 00:17:08.270 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I see.

00:17:08.270 --> 00:17:08.950 align:middle line:90%
I see.

00:17:08.950 --> 00:17:12.057 align:middle line:84%
And p to z is just an
arbitrary [INAUDIBLE].

00:17:12.057 --> 00:17:12.849 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

00:17:12.849 --> 00:17:14.738 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:17:14.738 --> 00:17:15.530 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

00:17:15.530 --> 00:17:20.150 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So that summation
there really represents--

00:17:20.150 --> 00:17:22.980 align:middle line:84%
you're trying to compute that
summation with a bunch of gates.

00:17:22.980 --> 00:17:23.730 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:17:23.730 --> 00:17:25.855 align:middle line:84%
So this is going-- this is
going to correspond to--

00:17:25.855 --> 00:17:26.355 align:middle line:90%
I'm sorry.

00:17:26.355 --> 00:17:27.180 align:middle line:90%
This is really bad.

00:17:27.180 --> 00:17:28.555 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, this is
going to correspond

00:17:28.555 --> 00:17:31.490 align:middle line:84%
to a pretty big circuit
here that's going

00:17:31.490 --> 00:17:33.240 align:middle line:84%
to look at the previous
layer, and-- yeah.

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:35.448 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So the depth of
U could be, like, the depth

00:17:35.448 --> 00:17:36.770 align:middle line:90%
of that mini circuit times p.

00:17:36.770 --> 00:17:37.340 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Exactly.

00:17:37.340 --> 00:17:38.300 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's perfect.

00:17:38.300 --> 00:17:49.170 align:middle line:90%
So-- yeah.

00:17:49.170 --> 00:17:50.765 align:middle line:84%
So I don't know
what your name is.

00:17:50.765 --> 00:17:51.390 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Gabe.

00:17:51.390 --> 00:17:52.182 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Gabe.

00:17:52.182 --> 00:17:55.440 align:middle line:84%
So as Gabe said, the depth of
the circuit is going to be--

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:58.770 align:middle line:84%
the depth of the original
circuit of C times

00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:00.748 align:middle line:90%
this depth here.

00:18:00.748 --> 00:18:02.540 align:middle line:84%
So the question is,
what is the depth here?

00:18:02.540 --> 00:18:05.920 align:middle line:90%


00:18:05.920 --> 00:18:06.420 align:middle line:90%
Thoughts?

00:18:06.420 --> 00:18:11.510 align:middle line:90%


00:18:11.510 --> 00:18:12.240 align:middle line:90%
Roughly.

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:12.782 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:18:12.782 --> 00:18:13.380 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]

00:18:13.380 --> 00:18:16.260 align:middle line:90%


00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:16.933 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: log S?

00:18:16.933 --> 00:18:17.850 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah OK.

00:18:17.850 --> 00:18:20.727 align:middle line:84%
Something has log S. Like,
polylog, maybe, at worse.

00:18:20.727 --> 00:18:30.180 align:middle line:90%


00:18:30.180 --> 00:18:33.240 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, and the reason is because
you can think of this big sum

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:37.890 align:middle line:90%
as a binary tree of sums.

00:18:37.890 --> 00:18:40.420 align:middle line:84%
And then at the very
bottom of this binary tree,

00:18:40.420 --> 00:18:45.095 align:middle line:84%
you have poly S many
things you're looking at.

00:18:45.095 --> 00:18:48.208 align:middle line:90%


00:18:48.208 --> 00:18:50.250 align:middle line:84%
So this entire depth,
because it's a binary tree,

00:18:50.250 --> 00:18:53.240 align:middle line:84%
is only going to be to
depth log S or something.

00:18:53.240 --> 00:18:54.860 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: What's U?

00:18:54.860 --> 00:18:55.950 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: The depths
of the universal circuit.

00:18:55.950 --> 00:18:56.180 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:18:56.180 --> 00:18:56.680 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:18:56.680 --> 00:18:57.440 align:middle line:90%
U.

00:18:57.440 --> 00:18:59.710 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

00:18:59.710 --> 00:19:02.220 align:middle line:84%
And what about the size
of this universal circuit?

00:19:02.220 --> 00:19:10.410 align:middle line:90%


00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:12.045 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:12.045 --> 00:19:13.420 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG:
Yeah, so it's going

00:19:13.420 --> 00:19:15.730 align:middle line:84%
to be the size of
original circuit times,

00:19:15.730 --> 00:19:20.460 align:middle line:84%
like, poly S, so it's
just going to be poly S.

00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:22.350 align:middle line:84%
And the reason for that
is because the size

00:19:22.350 --> 00:19:24.720 align:middle line:84%
of this universal circuit
is basically going to be--

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:26.500 align:middle line:84%
for every gate of
the original circuit,

00:19:26.500 --> 00:19:29.960 align:middle line:84%
you have-- this poly S-size
circuit's replacing it.

00:19:29.960 --> 00:19:32.100 align:middle line:84%
So you get to poly
S size here and then

00:19:32.100 --> 00:19:36.820 align:middle line:84%
times the size of the
circuit, which is poly S.

00:19:36.820 --> 00:19:37.320 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:19:37.320 --> 00:19:40.050 align:middle line:84%
So this is going to be like
part of the circuit we're

00:19:40.050 --> 00:19:41.670 align:middle line:84%
going to end up
using to delegate,

00:19:41.670 --> 00:19:44.040 align:middle line:84%
instead of using the
original circuit, C.

00:19:44.040 --> 00:19:47.430 align:middle line:84%
I guess the last claim that I
made is that this circuit U is

00:19:47.430 --> 00:19:50.435 align:middle line:84%
much simpler to describe
than the circuit C.

00:19:50.435 --> 00:19:51.810 align:middle line:84%
So particularly,
the add and mult

00:19:51.810 --> 00:19:57.430 align:middle line:84%
gates are easily computable
or describable, very simply.

00:19:57.430 --> 00:20:01.185 align:middle line:84%
And maybe let's look at this
and think about it for a minute.

00:20:01.185 --> 00:20:05.130 align:middle line:90%


00:20:05.130 --> 00:20:09.270 align:middle line:84%
So I guess if you're
trying to figure out--

00:20:09.270 --> 00:20:11.550 align:middle line:84%
I have-- I'm trying to
look at this specific gate,

00:20:11.550 --> 00:20:13.758 align:middle line:84%
and I want to see if it's,
like, an add or mult gate.

00:20:13.758 --> 00:20:16.210 align:middle line:84%
But you do, and you figure out
where in this tree you are.

00:20:16.210 --> 00:20:18.948 align:middle line:84%
And you can be like, oh, look,
I'm part of this binary tree.

00:20:18.948 --> 00:20:19.990 align:middle line:90%
It should be an add gate.

00:20:19.990 --> 00:20:21.740 align:middle line:84%
And then you're like,
OK, I'm an add gate.

00:20:21.740 --> 00:20:23.350 align:middle line:84%
Or you can read,
maybe, in the leaves.

00:20:23.350 --> 00:20:26.130 align:middle line:84%
You're like, oh, this
particular gate corresponds

00:20:26.130 --> 00:20:31.762 align:middle line:84%
to some add gate in the original
circuit times some values.

00:20:31.762 --> 00:20:34.470 align:middle line:84%
And you figure out-- oh, this is
supposed to be multiplied or add

00:20:34.470 --> 00:20:35.810 align:middle line:90%
gate or something.

00:20:35.810 --> 00:20:37.810 align:middle line:84%
The point is that this
is a pretty easy function

00:20:37.810 --> 00:20:38.560 align:middle line:90%
to describe.

00:20:38.560 --> 00:20:45.460 align:middle line:84%
In particular-- so there's going
to be low-depth Boolean formulas

00:20:45.460 --> 00:20:48.430 align:middle line:84%
that compute both the
add and mult gates.

00:20:48.430 --> 00:20:54.050 align:middle line:84%
So there's going to be
a Boolean formula of--

00:20:54.050 --> 00:20:54.580 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

00:20:54.580 --> 00:21:02.020 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to be a
Boolean formula of size

00:21:02.020 --> 00:21:16.460 align:middle line:84%
polylog S that computes the
add and mult functionalities.

00:21:16.460 --> 00:21:19.610 align:middle line:90%


00:21:19.610 --> 00:21:21.140 align:middle line:90%
So it tells you that--

00:21:21.140 --> 00:21:24.590 align:middle line:84%
within U, these--
because they're

00:21:24.590 --> 00:21:27.980 align:middle line:84%
small formulas that tell you
whether a particular gate in U

00:21:27.980 --> 00:21:29.920 align:middle line:90%
is an add or mult gate.

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:32.780 align:middle line:90%
That good?

00:21:32.780 --> 00:21:38.850 align:middle line:84%
Cool And so now,
like these formulas

00:21:38.850 --> 00:21:42.720 align:middle line:84%
are much easier to compute
than the previous add and mult.

00:21:42.720 --> 00:21:46.590 align:middle line:84%
So ostensibly, the verifier
can do it themselves now.

00:21:46.590 --> 00:21:48.295 align:middle line:90%
So--

00:21:48.295 --> 00:21:49.170 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:49.170 --> 00:21:49.995 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

00:21:49.995 --> 00:21:51.962 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Can you
use [INAUDIBLE]?

00:21:51.962 --> 00:21:52.670 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: OK.

00:21:52.670 --> 00:21:55.030 align:middle line:90%
So I guess, originally--

00:21:55.030 --> 00:21:57.280 align:middle line:84%
yeah, originally-- so how
do you compute add and mult?

00:21:57.280 --> 00:21:59.330 align:middle line:84%
We're going to-- I
didn't write it yet,

00:21:59.330 --> 00:22:03.800 align:middle line:84%
but you're going to assume that
z is a logspace-uniform circuit,

00:22:03.800 --> 00:22:07.750 align:middle line:84%
which means that you-- you
can do maybe up to time poly S

00:22:07.750 --> 00:22:11.590 align:middle line:84%
computation to compute
it, which is too much.

00:22:11.590 --> 00:22:14.980 align:middle line:84%
Here, since the circuit-- the
formula is only of size polylog,

00:22:14.980 --> 00:22:17.348 align:middle line:90%
you can compute it yourself.

00:22:17.348 --> 00:22:19.640 align:middle line:84%
So then when you're doing
GKR in the universal circuit,

00:22:19.640 --> 00:22:21.760 align:middle line:84%
you just have to do a small
computation as opposed

00:22:21.760 --> 00:22:23.900 align:middle line:90%
to the big one.

00:22:23.900 --> 00:22:24.400 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:22:24.400 --> 00:22:26.192 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Are these--
if we add [INAUDIBLE]

00:22:26.192 --> 00:22:28.450 align:middle line:90%
mults it's a logspace uniform?

00:22:28.450 --> 00:22:31.875 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: I guess maybe a
log logspace uniform, right?

00:22:31.875 --> 00:22:33.588 align:middle line:90%
Is that how it works?

00:22:33.588 --> 00:22:38.290 align:middle line:84%
[LAUGHS] I mean, there's
is polylog, right?

00:22:38.290 --> 00:22:39.700 align:middle line:84%
So basically, what
I mean by this

00:22:39.700 --> 00:22:43.990 align:middle line:84%
is you can feed in any
p omega 1 and omega 2

00:22:43.990 --> 00:22:45.640 align:middle line:84%
and then compute a
formula of this size

00:22:45.640 --> 00:22:48.250 align:middle line:84%
and it'll tell you
what the answer is.

00:22:48.250 --> 00:22:49.655 align:middle line:90%
So it's only polylog size.

00:22:49.655 --> 00:22:50.530 align:middle line:90%
So you only have to--

00:22:50.530 --> 00:22:52.030 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Does there
exist a formula

00:22:52.030 --> 00:22:53.838 align:middle line:84%
such that, for all
p1, omega 1, omega 2--

00:22:53.838 --> 00:22:54.630 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

00:22:54.630 --> 00:22:56.588 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: --the formula
computes it, correctly?

00:22:56.588 --> 00:22:57.840 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, yeah.

00:22:57.840 --> 00:22:58.215 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Right.

00:22:58.215 --> 00:22:58.715 align:middle line:90%
But-- yeah.

00:22:58.715 --> 00:23:01.140 align:middle line:84%
The naive way I would do it
is advice of polylog size.

00:23:01.140 --> 00:23:03.390 align:middle line:84%
But I'm assuming there's a
way to make it-- to get rid

00:23:03.390 --> 00:23:04.870 align:middle line:90%
of this advice, right?

00:23:04.870 --> 00:23:07.130 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]

00:23:07.130 --> 00:23:10.150 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, I think
it's log, log uniform.

00:23:10.150 --> 00:23:13.070 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think it's
just, like, [? max ?]

00:23:13.070 --> 00:23:15.890 align:middle line:84%
If-- to figure out what
level i is to figure out what

00:23:15.890 --> 00:23:17.050 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE] is.

00:23:17.050 --> 00:23:18.880 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
universal circuit.

00:23:18.880 --> 00:23:19.500 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, that's right.

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:20.310 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that is.

00:23:20.310 --> 00:23:32.988 align:middle line:90%


00:23:32.988 --> 00:23:35.030 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Oh, the size
for the universal circuit.

00:23:35.030 --> 00:23:35.822 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

00:23:35.822 --> 00:23:36.390 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:23:36.390 --> 00:23:40.150 align:middle line:90%
OK, I understand.

00:23:40.150 --> 00:23:42.890 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, maybe-- just
to summarize what we've done,

00:23:42.890 --> 00:23:45.020 align:middle line:84%
we've essentially
replaced this C,

00:23:45.020 --> 00:23:47.650 align:middle line:84%
which has complicated add
and mult gate description,

00:23:47.650 --> 00:23:49.906 align:middle line:84%
with a universal circuit,
which has a much simpler add

00:23:49.906 --> 00:23:50.989 align:middle line:90%
and mult gate description.

00:23:50.989 --> 00:23:52.531 align:middle line:84%
And then, now, what
we're going to do

00:23:52.531 --> 00:23:55.090 align:middle line:84%
is we're going to run GKR on
the universal circuit instead

00:23:55.090 --> 00:23:57.755 align:middle line:84%
of the original circuit, C.
The output is still the same

00:23:57.755 --> 00:24:00.130 align:middle line:84%
because the universal circuit
will simulate C and compute

00:24:00.130 --> 00:24:00.940 align:middle line:90%
C of x.

00:24:00.940 --> 00:24:03.790 align:middle line:84%
But now, the verifier
can, themselves,

00:24:03.790 --> 00:24:06.272 align:middle line:90%
compute. add and mult.

00:24:06.272 --> 00:24:09.540 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But the input [? N ?]
would increase because you

00:24:09.540 --> 00:24:10.920 align:middle line:90%
have to write the description.

00:24:10.920 --> 00:24:12.410 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Good, good.

00:24:12.410 --> 00:24:13.050 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, OK.

00:24:13.050 --> 00:24:18.913 align:middle line:90%


00:24:18.913 --> 00:24:24.212 align:middle line:84%
So now, we're going to run a
CPR on U. You can have U of C

00:24:24.212 --> 00:24:25.420 align:middle line:90%
because it's on the circuit--

00:24:25.420 --> 00:24:31.110 align:middle line:84%
this is U, instead
of C. So issues.

00:24:31.110 --> 00:24:33.325 align:middle line:90%


00:24:33.325 --> 00:24:34.450 align:middle line:90%
Maybe I should also write--

00:24:34.450 --> 00:24:54.830 align:middle line:90%


00:24:54.830 --> 00:24:56.100 align:middle line:90%
So issues.

00:24:56.100 --> 00:24:57.635 align:middle line:90%
So one is that--

00:24:57.635 --> 00:24:59.130 align:middle line:90%
that Arnan pointed out--

00:24:59.130 --> 00:25:01.315 align:middle line:84%
which is that the input
is now really large.

00:25:01.315 --> 00:25:04.640 align:middle line:90%


00:25:04.640 --> 00:25:07.045 align:middle line:90%
So issue one-- input is large.

00:25:07.045 --> 00:25:21.050 align:middle line:90%


00:25:21.050 --> 00:25:23.980 align:middle line:84%
And if you remember
from the GKR protocol,

00:25:23.980 --> 00:25:26.880 align:middle line:84%
the verifier at the very end
has to compute some points

00:25:26.880 --> 00:25:29.650 align:middle line:84%
in the low-degree
extension of the input.

00:25:29.650 --> 00:25:31.948 align:middle line:84%
Before, we said, OK, the
input is only size n.

00:25:31.948 --> 00:25:33.490 align:middle line:84%
So they can compute
it by themselves.

00:25:33.490 --> 00:25:36.200 align:middle line:84%
It's only n times polylog
time to compute it.

00:25:36.200 --> 00:25:41.100 align:middle line:84%
But now the input also has
C, which is size S. So now

00:25:41.100 --> 00:25:45.270 align:middle line:84%
you have to actually spend
time poly S to compute

00:25:45.270 --> 00:25:47.868 align:middle line:90%
the final layer, which is bad.

00:25:47.868 --> 00:25:49.660 align:middle line:84%
Like, we want the
verifier to be efficient.

00:25:49.660 --> 00:25:52.210 align:middle line:90%
Now we're back to square one.

00:25:52.210 --> 00:25:54.450 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
deal with this.

00:25:54.450 --> 00:25:55.335 align:middle line:90%
Any other issues?

00:25:55.335 --> 00:26:03.363 align:middle line:90%


00:26:03.363 --> 00:26:04.030 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Sorry.

00:26:04.030 --> 00:26:08.313 align:middle line:84%
In the GKR protocol, the add
times poly S's, do I only

00:26:08.313 --> 00:26:10.480 align:middle line:84%
need to compute a couple
of bits in the input layer,

00:26:10.480 --> 00:26:12.615 align:middle line:84%
or do I need to compute
the entire input layer?

00:26:12.615 --> 00:26:13.990 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: You
need to read it

00:26:13.990 --> 00:26:15.340 align:middle line:84%
all because what
you're going to do

00:26:15.340 --> 00:26:17.090 align:middle line:84%
is you're going to
look at the input layer

00:26:17.090 --> 00:26:18.850 align:middle line:84%
and then compute the
low-degree extension.

00:26:18.850 --> 00:26:19.900 align:middle line:84%
And the only way
you can do something

00:26:19.900 --> 00:26:20.935 align:middle line:84%
with the low-degree
extension-- if you

00:26:20.935 --> 00:26:22.570 align:middle line:90%
know everything in the coding.

00:26:22.570 --> 00:26:29.433 align:middle line:90%
Otherwise-- yeah Yeah.

00:26:29.433 --> 00:26:30.100 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Sorry.

00:26:30.100 --> 00:26:31.058 align:middle line:90%
I just have a question.

00:26:31.058 --> 00:26:33.580 align:middle line:84%
Before you [? have ?]
issues, is it obvious

00:26:33.580 --> 00:26:36.540 align:middle line:84%
that the verifier can compute
like add and mult tilde, given

00:26:36.540 --> 00:26:39.120 align:middle line:90%
that you can add, but like--?

00:26:39.120 --> 00:26:40.740 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Good, good.

00:26:40.740 --> 00:26:41.350 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Oh, is that the issue?

00:26:41.350 --> 00:26:41.970 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

00:26:41.970 --> 00:26:42.470 align:middle line:90%
So--

00:26:42.470 --> 00:26:43.758 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHS]

00:26:43.758 --> 00:26:52.018 align:middle line:90%


00:26:52.018 --> 00:26:52.810 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

00:26:52.810 --> 00:26:54.250 align:middle line:84%
So Nikon's question
is, how do you

00:26:54.250 --> 00:26:56.950 align:middle line:84%
compute the low-degree
extension of add and mult?

00:26:56.950 --> 00:26:59.680 align:middle line:84%
Right now, I've only given you a
Boolean formula to compute them.

00:26:59.680 --> 00:27:02.115 align:middle line:84%
So you can compute the
values on the hypercube,

00:27:02.115 --> 00:27:03.490 align:middle line:84%
but you can't
necessarily compute

00:27:03.490 --> 00:27:05.170 align:middle line:90%
the logic extensions of them.

00:27:05.170 --> 00:27:07.640 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to see how
to fix both of these things.

00:27:07.640 --> 00:27:10.140 align:middle line:84%
And hopefully, if I'm honest,
these are the only two issues.

00:27:10.140 --> 00:27:11.070 align:middle line:90%
So we'll see.

00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:13.820 align:middle line:90%


00:27:13.820 --> 00:27:14.905 align:middle line:90%
Is this OK so far?

00:27:14.905 --> 00:27:32.165 align:middle line:90%


00:27:32.165 --> 00:27:35.390 align:middle line:84%
So issue one-- the
input is now all

00:27:35.390 --> 00:27:37.620 align:middle line:84%
of the circuit, plus
the original input,

00:27:37.620 --> 00:27:39.807 align:middle line:90%
so it's very large.

00:27:39.807 --> 00:27:41.390 align:middle line:84%
So right now, this
is what our circuit

00:27:41.390 --> 00:27:48.300 align:middle line:84%
looks like-- is U. Big part
is C And small part is x.

00:27:48.300 --> 00:27:51.260 align:middle line:84%
So here's when the
logspace uniformity of C

00:27:51.260 --> 00:27:52.120 align:middle line:90%
is going to come in.

00:27:52.120 --> 00:27:56.120 align:middle line:90%


00:27:56.120 --> 00:27:58.880 align:middle line:84%
So if you ever read
GKR statements,

00:27:58.880 --> 00:28:01.160 align:middle line:84%
the theorem only
holds for when C

00:28:01.160 --> 00:28:03.320 align:middle line:84%
is logspace-uniform,
which means that there's

00:28:03.320 --> 00:28:04.945 align:middle line:90%
some small description of it.

00:28:04.945 --> 00:28:08.900 align:middle line:90%


00:28:08.900 --> 00:28:18.630 align:middle line:84%
So some small log S
size description of C.

00:28:18.630 --> 00:28:23.400 align:middle line:84%
And maybe let's call
this description this.

00:28:23.400 --> 00:28:25.770 align:middle line:90%
And this is Turing machine M--

00:28:25.770 --> 00:28:29.530 align:middle line:90%


00:28:29.530 --> 00:28:34.610 align:middle line:84%
that, basically, if it
reads in this description,

00:28:34.610 --> 00:28:38.910 align:middle line:84%
it can then output like
the entire circuit C.

00:28:38.910 --> 00:28:45.970 align:middle line:84%
And this Turing machine
should run in logspace.

00:28:45.970 --> 00:28:47.790 align:middle line:84%
But it can run for
as much as S time.

00:28:47.790 --> 00:28:55.270 align:middle line:90%


00:28:55.270 --> 00:28:56.940 align:middle line:90%
So C is too big.

00:28:56.940 --> 00:28:57.700 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:28:57.700 --> 00:28:59.560 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: So M is, like, fixed.

00:28:59.560 --> 00:29:00.820 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, M is--

00:29:00.820 --> 00:29:02.510 align:middle line:84%
yeah, it's just a-- it's
fixed Turing machine.

00:29:02.510 --> 00:29:03.010 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:29:03.010 --> 00:29:06.080 align:middle line:90%


00:29:06.080 --> 00:29:06.580 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:29:06.580 --> 00:29:09.080 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Just to make sure,
intuitively, why this matters--

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:10.610 align:middle line:84%
it's because C has
to have, somehow,

00:29:10.610 --> 00:29:13.940 align:middle line:84%
nice structure for it to be
defined on inputs of different

00:29:13.940 --> 00:29:15.740 align:middle line:90%
lengths and stuff--

00:29:15.740 --> 00:29:18.125 align:middle line:90%
being non-uniform?

00:29:18.125 --> 00:29:20.750 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: I think one main
thing is that the verifier needs

00:29:20.750 --> 00:29:25.030 align:middle line:84%
to be able to know what C is
because like in order to get get

00:29:25.030 --> 00:29:26.520 align:middle line:84%
on the circuit C
for the verifier,

00:29:26.520 --> 00:29:29.370 align:middle line:84%
they need to have some way
of describing the circuit,

00:29:29.370 --> 00:29:30.270 align:middle line:90%
in some sense.

00:29:30.270 --> 00:29:33.980 align:middle line:84%
This is basically saying, the
circuit has to be like log size

00:29:33.980 --> 00:29:36.980 align:middle line:84%
so that the verifier can hold
it and have some way of knowing

00:29:36.980 --> 00:29:38.648 align:middle line:90%
what circuit C is.

00:29:38.648 --> 00:29:40.190 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I guess
I'm trying to think

00:29:40.190 --> 00:29:42.530 align:middle line:84%
about what kinds of circuits
are excluded by this--

00:29:42.530 --> 00:29:45.440 align:middle line:84%
circuits that-- basically,
you have to write out all--

00:29:45.440 --> 00:29:47.180 align:middle line:84%
the entire truth
table to describe.

00:29:47.180 --> 00:29:48.950 align:middle line:84%
Is that what we're
trying to get rid of?

00:29:48.950 --> 00:29:51.990 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: So I think Ted is
our local complexity theorist,

00:29:51.990 --> 00:29:52.760 align:middle line:90%
so I'm going to--

00:29:52.760 --> 00:29:53.720 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: This is
the door I want.

00:29:53.720 --> 00:29:55.178 align:middle line:84%
Basically, the
circuit [INAUDIBLE].

00:29:55.178 --> 00:29:58.270 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:29:58.270 --> 00:29:59.977 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:29:59.977 --> 00:30:01.810 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You can't
hardcode things, though.

00:30:01.810 --> 00:30:04.010 align:middle line:84%
You can't hardcode a random
string or something like that.

00:30:04.010 --> 00:30:04.677 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Right.

00:30:04.677 --> 00:30:07.370 align:middle line:90%


00:30:07.370 --> 00:30:09.728 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, [INAUDIBLE]
circuit [INAUDIBLE].

00:30:09.728 --> 00:30:13.810 align:middle line:90%


00:30:13.810 --> 00:30:15.070 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: So C is large.

00:30:15.070 --> 00:30:17.890 align:middle line:84%
And the problem was that reading
this entire thing is too much.

00:30:17.890 --> 00:30:20.440 align:middle line:84%
But we have a short
description of C.

00:30:20.440 --> 00:30:25.605 align:middle line:84%
So let's also, maybe, have a
circuit that computes C. Maybe

00:30:25.605 --> 00:30:26.980 align:middle line:84%
we'll call this
M just because we

00:30:26.980 --> 00:30:29.563 align:middle line:84%
have a Turing machine called M,
but it's not a Turing machine.

00:30:29.563 --> 00:30:30.250 align:middle line:90%
It's a circuit.

00:30:30.250 --> 00:30:34.330 align:middle line:84%
And this circuit will take,
as input, the description C,

00:30:34.330 --> 00:30:36.640 align:middle line:84%
with the things
around it, and then it

00:30:36.640 --> 00:30:39.520 align:middle line:84%
will output the entire
circuit description.

00:30:39.520 --> 00:30:40.780 align:middle line:84%
And that will feed into the
universal gate universal K ?]

00:30:40.780 --> 00:30:41.863 align:middle line:90%
that we just talked about.

00:30:41.863 --> 00:30:44.640 align:middle line:90%


00:30:44.640 --> 00:30:47.570 align:middle line:84%
So this circuit here
is our circuit C prime

00:30:47.570 --> 00:30:49.440 align:middle line:84%
that we will actually
be delegating on.

00:30:49.440 --> 00:30:56.590 align:middle line:90%


00:30:56.590 --> 00:30:58.820 align:middle line:84%
And just to be clear,
which ones are the inputs?

00:30:58.820 --> 00:31:00.580 align:middle line:90%
The inputs are this--

00:31:00.580 --> 00:31:02.750 align:middle line:84%
this description
of z and also x.

00:31:02.750 --> 00:31:05.620 align:middle line:84%
Like, this entire C here is
now wrapped into the circuit.

00:31:05.620 --> 00:31:07.060 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:31:07.060 --> 00:31:08.650 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I put the
notation-- like M,

00:31:08.650 --> 00:31:12.890 align:middle line:84%
parentheses, bracket C equals C.
So what do you mean by equal C?

00:31:12.890 --> 00:31:13.390 align:middle line:90%
Like--

00:31:13.390 --> 00:31:13.650 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

00:31:13.650 --> 00:31:15.250 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: --is there some other
description of the circuit,

00:31:15.250 --> 00:31:17.800 align:middle line:84%
or would say the Turing
machine is able to run

00:31:17.800 --> 00:31:20.170 align:middle line:90%
the circuit on any [INAUDIBLE]?

00:31:20.170 --> 00:31:24.070 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: So it will run on
log S space and up to S time,

00:31:24.070 --> 00:31:26.403 align:middle line:84%
and then it can output any
gate of the circuit you want.

00:31:26.403 --> 00:31:27.528 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Any gate of the--

00:31:27.528 --> 00:31:29.920 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, and maybe
if you have an output here

00:31:29.920 --> 00:31:32.427 align:middle line:84%
that's separate from its actual
space that you can compute

00:31:32.427 --> 00:31:35.860 align:middle line:84%
on compute on, ?] you can
just output the gate by one.

00:31:35.860 --> 00:31:37.000 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Oh, OK.

00:31:37.000 --> 00:31:39.548 align:middle line:84%
So you say equal C
means find the gate.

00:31:39.548 --> 00:31:40.340 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Sure.

00:31:40.340 --> 00:31:40.960 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:31:40.960 --> 00:31:43.390 align:middle line:84%
I'll put in the
description of C,

00:31:43.390 --> 00:31:47.040 align:middle line:84%
or description of the
gates of C So output gates.

00:31:47.040 --> 00:31:51.480 align:middle line:90%


00:31:51.480 --> 00:31:52.110 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:31:52.110 --> 00:31:53.527 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If you're
just unrolling

00:31:53.527 --> 00:31:55.458 align:middle line:90%
M, isn't that really deep?

00:31:55.458 --> 00:31:56.500 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Good, good.

00:31:56.500 --> 00:31:57.280 align:middle line:90%
OK, yeah.

00:31:57.280 --> 00:31:58.680 align:middle line:90%
So what's the depth of this?

00:31:58.680 --> 00:32:01.500 align:middle line:84%
So-- OK, M can run
in time up to S.

00:32:01.500 --> 00:32:03.487 align:middle line:84%
So this could, ostensibly,
be depth S, which

00:32:03.487 --> 00:32:05.070 align:middle line:84%
is bad because our
verifier would have

00:32:05.070 --> 00:32:06.800 align:middle line:90%
to depend on the depth also.

00:32:06.800 --> 00:32:09.660 align:middle line:90%


00:32:09.660 --> 00:32:12.150 align:middle line:84%
Well, it turns out you
can do it in depth log S.

00:32:12.150 --> 00:32:14.320 align:middle line:84%
And I'm not going to
tell you how to do it.

00:32:14.320 --> 00:32:16.153 align:middle line:84%
You're going to tell
me by the end of class.

00:32:16.153 --> 00:32:19.230 align:middle line:90%
So that's the goal, by 3:00 PM.

00:32:19.230 --> 00:32:27.020 align:middle line:84%
And this will also be size
S. So we'll get there.

00:32:27.020 --> 00:32:28.897 align:middle line:84%
But it'll be your
job to convince me.

00:32:28.897 --> 00:32:30.230 align:middle line:90%
It's not my job to convince you.

00:32:30.230 --> 00:32:33.720 align:middle line:90%


00:32:33.720 --> 00:32:34.220 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

00:32:34.220 --> 00:32:34.803 align:middle line:90%
Anything else?

00:32:34.803 --> 00:32:35.420 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:32:35.420 --> 00:32:37.350 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How do you turn this
Turing machine into a circuit?

00:32:37.350 --> 00:32:37.520 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Good.

00:32:37.520 --> 00:32:38.020 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:32:38.020 --> 00:32:38.940 align:middle line:90%
You'll tell me.

00:32:38.940 --> 00:32:43.640 align:middle line:90%


00:32:43.640 --> 00:32:45.910 align:middle line:84%
It's the weekend, so
the weekend don't worry.

00:32:45.910 --> 00:32:48.410 align:middle line:84%
I think you'll be able to figure
it out at the end of class.

00:32:48.410 --> 00:32:54.620 align:middle line:90%


00:32:54.620 --> 00:32:57.440 align:middle line:84%
And then the other
claim is that this is--

00:32:57.440 --> 00:32:59.340 align:middle line:90%
has easy add and mult gates.

00:32:59.340 --> 00:33:02.840 align:middle line:84%
And also-- you'll also tell
me that by the end of class.

00:33:02.840 --> 00:33:04.490 align:middle line:84%
So basically, the
circuit is going

00:33:04.490 --> 00:33:06.198 align:middle line:84%
to have all the nice
properties you want.

00:33:06.198 --> 00:33:08.840 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be depth, log S
size S. The whole circuit-- now,

00:33:08.840 --> 00:33:12.500 align:middle line:84%
C prime is going to be size poly
S because it was also size poly

00:33:12.500 --> 00:33:19.410 align:middle line:84%
S. This should probably be
poly S, not just size S.

00:33:19.410 --> 00:33:22.865 align:middle line:84%
And then total depth is going to
be, like, poly log S. So-- yeah.

00:33:22.865 --> 00:33:24.740 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The statement
is, like, truth table

00:33:24.740 --> 00:33:27.050 align:middle line:90%
of M on C equals C--

00:33:27.050 --> 00:33:30.821 align:middle line:84%
on bracket C equals
C. So do we need

00:33:30.821 --> 00:33:33.548 align:middle line:84%
size of C different copies
of M [INAUDIBLE] bits?

00:33:33.548 --> 00:33:34.340 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Sure.

00:33:34.340 --> 00:33:34.960 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yeah.

00:33:34.960 --> 00:33:35.460 align:middle line:90%
Sure.

00:33:35.460 --> 00:33:36.740 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
all of them?

00:33:36.740 --> 00:33:36.950 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:33:36.950 --> 00:33:37.742 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Sure.

00:33:37.742 --> 00:33:39.020 align:middle line:90%
Yeah or you can think--

00:33:39.020 --> 00:33:41.810 align:middle line:84%
it's a thing-- you can have an
output tape, where you can just

00:33:41.810 --> 00:33:43.740 align:middle line:84%
output everything
to, or another thing.

00:33:43.740 --> 00:33:44.450 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:33:44.450 --> 00:33:44.950 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:33:44.950 --> 00:33:48.458 align:middle line:84%
I just-- I want to make
sure that it doesn't break.

00:33:48.458 --> 00:33:49.250 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

00:33:49.250 --> 00:33:50.910 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: No, it doesn't break
[INAUDIBLE] for me either.

00:33:50.910 --> 00:33:52.220 align:middle line:90%
You can take it [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:52.220 --> 00:33:53.720 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: OK, yeah.

00:33:53.720 --> 00:33:55.803 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You might have
just said this, actually,

00:33:55.803 --> 00:33:58.490 align:middle line:84%
but [INAUDIBLE] on the line
that says log S size description

00:33:58.490 --> 00:33:59.570 align:middle line:90%
of C--

00:33:59.570 --> 00:34:00.450 align:middle line:90%
like, C in brackets.

00:34:00.450 --> 00:34:01.650 align:middle line:90%
Like, C in brackets is a string.

00:34:01.650 --> 00:34:03.233 align:middle line:84%
It's not actually,
like, log S, right?

00:34:03.233 --> 00:34:05.940 align:middle line:84%
It's, like-- S or
something, isn't it?

00:34:05.940 --> 00:34:06.440 align:middle line:90%
Or--

00:34:06.440 --> 00:34:08.565 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: No, this is
going to be a log S size.

00:34:08.565 --> 00:34:12.370 align:middle line:90%


00:34:12.370 --> 00:34:14.949 align:middle line:84%
So the circuit C is
size S, but there's

00:34:14.949 --> 00:34:18.130 align:middle line:84%
a short description that can
tell you what circuit C is

00:34:18.130 --> 00:34:20.592 align:middle line:90%
going to look like.

00:34:20.592 --> 00:34:22.550 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I guess we got
a family of circuits--

00:34:22.550 --> 00:34:23.900 align:middle line:90%
like, C1 through CM.

00:34:23.900 --> 00:34:26.449 align:middle line:84%
The description could be,
like, the number n that you

00:34:26.449 --> 00:34:28.719 align:middle line:90%
should print or something.

00:34:28.719 --> 00:34:29.808 align:middle line:90%
Like a copy of that.

00:34:29.808 --> 00:34:30.600 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Sure.

00:34:30.600 --> 00:34:31.100 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:34:31.100 --> 00:34:32.780 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Then why
is that tree there?

00:34:32.780 --> 00:34:39.206 align:middle line:84%
That log S-- wouldn't the
number of leaves just be S?

00:34:39.206 --> 00:34:40.050 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE] M tree.

00:34:40.050 --> 00:34:42.050 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: So this
description is size log S,

00:34:42.050 --> 00:34:46.190 align:middle line:84%
so this entire thing
here is log-S size.

00:34:46.190 --> 00:34:50.037 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Isn't that
a full [INAUDIBLE]?

00:34:50.037 --> 00:34:52.204 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: I guess it
goes like this, more than--

00:34:52.204 --> 00:34:53.393 align:middle line:90%
yes, Alex.

00:34:53.393 --> 00:34:55.653 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:34:55.653 --> 00:34:56.060 align:middle line:90%


00:34:56.060 --> 00:34:58.060 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yes, because
this is [INAUDIBLE] log S

00:34:58.060 --> 00:35:00.080 align:middle line:84%
and [INAUDIBLE] size log S.
It's roughly [? one ?] [? or ?]

00:35:00.080 --> 00:35:01.043 align:middle line:90%
[? two ?] [? things. ?]

00:35:01.043 --> 00:35:03.626 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] circuit
that everyone could [INAUDIBLE].

00:35:03.626 --> 00:35:05.928 align:middle line:90%


00:35:05.928 --> 00:35:07.220 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: There are conditions.

00:35:07.220 --> 00:35:09.088 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]

00:35:09.088 --> 00:35:11.130 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But we don't
know how to unroll it yet.

00:35:11.130 --> 00:35:12.865 align:middle line:84%
When we unroll it,
we might [INAUDIBLE].

00:35:12.865 --> 00:35:21.430 align:middle line:90%


00:35:21.430 --> 00:35:22.780 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Cool.

00:35:22.780 --> 00:35:25.480 align:middle line:84%
OK, so the claim is
given the circuit

00:35:25.480 --> 00:35:29.750 align:middle line:84%
and given all these properties
you can prove to me later about,

00:35:29.750 --> 00:35:32.750 align:middle line:90%
then you can do GKR.

00:35:32.750 --> 00:35:34.450 align:middle line:90%
So according to our--

00:35:34.450 --> 00:35:35.180 align:middle line:90%
I put it behind.

00:35:35.180 --> 00:35:36.390 align:middle line:90%
I should push it forward.

00:35:36.390 --> 00:35:38.140 align:middle line:84%
So the prover is going
to run in time poly

00:35:38.140 --> 00:35:40.900 align:middle line:84%
S, where S is the size of
this new circuit, C prime.

00:35:40.900 --> 00:35:42.920 align:middle line:84%
This is size poly S.
This is size poly.

00:35:42.920 --> 00:35:47.170 align:middle line:84%
So everything is poly S, and
poly of polys, polys of poly S

00:35:47.170 --> 00:35:48.700 align:middle line:90%
for the prover.

00:35:48.700 --> 00:35:52.510 align:middle line:84%
And the verifier is the depth
of the circuit, plus the input

00:35:52.510 --> 00:35:54.670 align:middle line:90%
length.

00:35:54.670 --> 00:35:56.650 align:middle line:84%
Steps here-- this is
going to be, like--

00:35:56.650 --> 00:35:59.050 align:middle line:84%
I think we said
it was, basically,

00:35:59.050 --> 00:36:00.820 align:middle line:84%
depth of the original
circuit times log.

00:36:00.820 --> 00:36:02.540 align:middle line:84%
And this is going
to be really small.

00:36:02.540 --> 00:36:04.623 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be, like,
log S So it's going to be,

00:36:04.623 --> 00:36:07.090 align:middle line:84%
overall, the same depth
of the original circuit.

00:36:07.090 --> 00:36:09.383 align:middle line:84%
And then the input size
is now log S plus n.

00:36:09.383 --> 00:36:10.800 align:middle line:84%
So it's not so
much bigger either.

00:36:10.800 --> 00:36:22.810 align:middle line:90%


00:36:22.810 --> 00:36:24.430 align:middle line:90%
OK, input.

00:36:24.430 --> 00:36:26.717 align:middle line:90%
Maybe-- yeah, OK.

00:36:26.717 --> 00:36:28.550 align:middle line:84%
So the second issue
that Nikon pointed out--

00:36:28.550 --> 00:36:31.720 align:middle line:84%
how do you compute the
low-degree extensions of the add

00:36:31.720 --> 00:36:34.730 align:middle line:84%
and mult gates, whereas we only
currently have Boolean formulas

00:36:34.730 --> 00:36:36.422 align:middle line:90%
that compute them on the--

00:36:36.422 --> 00:36:38.630 align:middle line:84%
I guess, in this case, it's
even a Boolean hypercube.

00:36:38.630 --> 00:36:40.130 align:middle line:90%
It's not even the h- hypercube.

00:36:40.130 --> 00:36:40.630 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:36:40.630 --> 00:36:41.130 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:36:41.130 --> 00:36:41.630 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [?

00:36:41.630 --> 00:36:43.900 align:middle line:84%
Wait, I got another ?]
[? question. ?] [INAUDIBLE]

00:36:43.900 --> 00:36:46.900 align:middle line:84%
So the idea here was you wanted
to ultimately get the bottom

00:36:46.900 --> 00:36:49.270 align:middle line:90%
layer to be size small?

00:36:49.270 --> 00:36:51.670 align:middle line:84%
But still, this middle
layer that you get to that

00:36:51.670 --> 00:36:54.000 align:middle line:84%
has C written on it when
you run the algorithm.

00:36:54.000 --> 00:36:55.000 align:middle line:90%
That's big.

00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:57.280 align:middle line:84%
So at some point, you
have to be able to get

00:36:57.280 --> 00:36:59.368 align:middle line:84%
low-degree evaluations
in this middle layer?

00:36:59.368 --> 00:37:00.910 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: The
prover would do it.

00:37:00.910 --> 00:37:02.653 align:middle line:90%
You would never have to do it.

00:37:02.653 --> 00:37:03.320 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I see.

00:37:03.320 --> 00:37:06.020 align:middle line:90%


00:37:06.020 --> 00:37:08.450 align:middle line:84%
So the GKR-- the
protocol we had last time

00:37:08.450 --> 00:37:10.580 align:middle line:84%
was only for circuits
with extensions

00:37:10.580 --> 00:37:11.870 align:middle line:90%
that get smaller, right?

00:37:11.870 --> 00:37:13.700 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG:
[INAUDIBLE] Doesn't

00:37:13.700 --> 00:37:15.410 align:middle line:84%
matter, because
what-- the way I did

00:37:15.410 --> 00:37:18.683 align:middle line:84%
is she extended every
layer to size S anyway.

00:37:18.683 --> 00:37:20.100 align:middle line:84%
So even if it
wasn't S originally,

00:37:20.100 --> 00:37:22.160 align:middle line:90%
she extended it to be big.

00:37:22.160 --> 00:37:24.710 align:middle line:84%
So I think, sometimes, S can
be viewed as an upper bound

00:37:24.710 --> 00:37:25.930 align:middle line:90%
of each layer size, also.

00:37:25.930 --> 00:37:32.100 align:middle line:90%


00:37:32.100 --> 00:37:36.257 align:middle line:84%
So second issue-- how do
we compute add and mult?

00:37:36.257 --> 00:37:38.590 align:middle line:84%
So we have-- let me just write
down what I put up there.

00:37:38.590 --> 00:37:39.382 align:middle line:90%
I can push this up.

00:37:39.382 --> 00:37:43.055 align:middle line:84%
So we have these two
Boolean formulas.

00:37:43.055 --> 00:38:04.565 align:middle line:90%


00:38:04.565 --> 00:38:06.190 align:middle line:84%
And it's going to
compute add and mult.

00:38:06.190 --> 00:38:07.890 align:middle line:84%
This doesn't have a go on top
because it's not a low-degree ?]

00:38:07.890 --> 00:38:08.590 align:middle line:90%
extension.

00:38:08.590 --> 00:38:32.270 align:middle line:90%


00:38:32.270 --> 00:38:34.500 align:middle line:84%
So, So this ?] is what
phi of add looks like.

00:38:34.500 --> 00:38:37.540 align:middle line:84%
It's a Boolean formula,
so it's, like, a big tree.

00:38:37.540 --> 00:38:38.290 align:middle line:90%
Not big, actually.

00:38:38.290 --> 00:38:39.207 align:middle line:90%
It's very small trees.

00:38:39.207 --> 00:38:41.570 align:middle line:90%
It's, like, polylog size.

00:38:41.570 --> 00:38:43.660 align:middle line:90%
I know output at the end.

00:38:43.660 --> 00:38:47.600 align:middle line:90%


00:38:47.600 --> 00:38:50.990 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, what you're like--

00:38:50.990 --> 00:38:53.750 align:middle line:84%
yeah, out of b1
option something.

00:38:53.750 --> 00:38:57.130 align:middle line:84%
So here's-- the
inputs are all bits.

00:38:57.130 --> 00:38:59.315 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to
be 0, 1 to the--

00:38:59.315 --> 00:39:01.860 align:middle line:90%


00:39:01.860 --> 00:39:03.835 align:middle line:84%
I guess this should
be taking as input.

00:39:03.835 --> 00:39:04.960 align:middle line:90%
So the size of the circuit.

00:39:04.960 --> 00:39:07.230 align:middle line:84%
And telling you whether or
not that particular gate

00:39:07.230 --> 00:39:08.605 align:middle line:84%
is add or mult
gate, so it should

00:39:08.605 --> 00:39:13.972 align:middle line:84%
be like size of like log n
of the size of the C prime.

00:39:13.972 --> 00:39:15.080 align:middle line:90%
So this should be--

00:39:15.080 --> 00:39:25.430 align:middle line:90%


00:39:25.430 --> 00:39:25.930 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:39:25.930 --> 00:39:28.990 align:middle line:90%


00:39:28.990 --> 00:39:29.490 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

00:39:29.490 --> 00:39:31.233 align:middle line:90%
And our goal is to compute--

00:39:31.233 --> 00:39:38.110 align:middle line:90%


00:39:38.110 --> 00:39:40.780 align:middle line:90%
and so, here, we have--

00:39:40.780 --> 00:39:44.560 align:middle line:84%
H is going to be an
H. And so here, M

00:39:44.560 --> 00:39:54.237 align:middle line:90%
is log base H of S prime.

00:39:54.237 --> 00:39:56.320 align:middle line:84%
So this is what we want,
and this is what we have.

00:39:56.320 --> 00:40:00.350 align:middle line:90%


00:40:00.350 --> 00:40:01.698 align:middle line:90%
I want to repeat again.

00:40:01.698 --> 00:40:03.240 align:middle line:84%
This is not the
low-degree extension.

00:40:03.240 --> 00:40:05.160 align:middle line:84%
This is going to be a
low-degree extension.

00:40:05.160 --> 00:40:07.770 align:middle line:90%


00:40:07.770 --> 00:40:14.530 align:middle line:84%
So what do we do for sharp set
or sharpie Do you guys remember?

00:40:14.530 --> 00:40:15.090 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:40:15.090 --> 00:40:15.420 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Sorry.

00:40:15.420 --> 00:40:16.380 align:middle line:90%
One more question.

00:40:16.380 --> 00:40:18.420 align:middle line:84%
Is a low-degree
extension-- but aren't

00:40:18.420 --> 00:40:20.820 align:middle line:90%
low-degree extensions unique?

00:40:20.820 --> 00:40:24.550 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: So it's a
extension that has low degree,

00:40:24.550 --> 00:40:25.540 align:middle line:90%
but it's not that low.

00:40:25.540 --> 00:40:26.280 align:middle line:90%
It's, like, somewhat low.

00:40:26.280 --> 00:40:27.280 align:middle line:90%
It's low to [INAUDIBLE].

00:40:27.280 --> 00:40:29.860 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So we're talking about
just extension [INAUDIBLE].

00:40:29.860 --> 00:40:30.660 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

00:40:30.660 --> 00:40:32.452 align:middle line:84%
And it's not going to
be the lowest degree,

00:40:32.452 --> 00:40:34.600 align:middle line:84%
but it will have
low enough degree We

00:40:34.600 --> 00:40:35.850 align:middle line:90%
can call it low enough degree.

00:40:35.850 --> 00:40:36.350 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:36.350 --> 00:40:39.820 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, low enough
degree extension.

00:40:39.820 --> 00:40:45.312 align:middle line:90%


00:40:45.312 --> 00:40:47.663 align:middle line:90%
So you can call this the LEID.

00:40:47.663 --> 00:40:51.298 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:51.298 --> 00:40:57.770 align:middle line:90%


00:40:57.770 --> 00:40:58.270 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:40:58.270 --> 00:40:59.810 align:middle line:84%
So it's not going to be
the low-degree extension,

00:40:59.810 --> 00:41:00.970 align:middle line:90%
but it will be kind of.

00:41:00.970 --> 00:41:03.250 align:middle line:90%
It'll be OK.

00:41:03.250 --> 00:41:06.063 align:middle line:84%
So what did we do for
sharpie the other--

00:41:06.063 --> 00:41:06.980 align:middle line:90%
like, the first class.

00:41:06.980 --> 00:41:10.510 align:middle line:84%
This is, like, going
three weeks ago.

00:41:10.510 --> 00:41:12.580 align:middle line:84%
The idea there, we took
a Boolean formula--

00:41:12.580 --> 00:41:15.085 align:middle line:90%
or a formula, right?

00:41:15.085 --> 00:41:16.460 align:middle line:84%
OK, let me maybe
jog your memory.

00:41:16.460 --> 00:41:20.080 align:middle line:84%
So the idea was you want to
count the number of satisfied

00:41:20.080 --> 00:41:22.690 align:middle line:90%
equations through some of CSP.

00:41:22.690 --> 00:41:26.350 align:middle line:84%
You first turn this into
some Boolean formula.

00:41:26.350 --> 00:41:29.507 align:middle line:90%
And then you do something to it.

00:41:29.507 --> 00:41:31.340 align:middle line:84%
And then you can apply
the subject protocol.

00:41:31.340 --> 00:41:32.640 align:middle line:90%
So what do you do in the middle?

00:41:32.640 --> 00:41:33.170 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Arithmetize.

00:41:33.170 --> 00:41:34.253 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Arithmetize.

00:41:34.253 --> 00:41:35.785 align:middle line:90%
Let's arithmetize is this thing.

00:41:35.785 --> 00:41:37.410 align:middle line:84%
So maybe, first,
before we arithmetize,

00:41:37.410 --> 00:41:38.930 align:middle line:84%
we should change
the input formats.

00:41:38.930 --> 00:41:40.138 align:middle line:90%
So over here, we have h here.

00:41:40.138 --> 00:41:42.170 align:middle line:90%
We have Boolean, like 0, 1.

00:41:42.170 --> 00:41:43.820 align:middle line:84%
But what you can
do is you can just

00:41:43.820 --> 00:41:47.240 align:middle line:90%
take maybe a chunk of size--

00:41:47.240 --> 00:41:48.990 align:middle line:84%
sorry, this is a like
really minor detail.

00:41:48.990 --> 00:41:50.460 align:middle line:90%
I think-- it's fine.

00:41:50.460 --> 00:41:51.500 align:middle line:90%
Let's do it anyway.

00:41:51.500 --> 00:41:52.930 align:middle line:90%
So this is going to be size--

00:41:52.930 --> 00:41:55.570 align:middle line:90%


00:41:55.570 --> 00:41:57.050 align:middle line:90%
log of size of h.

00:41:57.050 --> 00:41:59.560 align:middle line:84%
And then-- so this is how many
bits are going to be here.

00:41:59.560 --> 00:42:01.030 align:middle line:90%
And you're going to take h.

00:42:01.030 --> 00:42:04.900 align:middle line:84%
And you can convert it
via some polynomial.

00:42:04.900 --> 00:42:11.330 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be a
degree h minus 1 poly.

00:42:11.330 --> 00:42:16.040 align:middle line:84%
It's going to convert any
element, h, of your bigger

00:42:16.040 --> 00:42:20.608 align:middle line:90%
set, capital H, to 0 string.

00:42:20.608 --> 00:42:22.150 align:middle line:84%
And you can do this
a bunch of times.

00:42:22.150 --> 00:42:31.640 align:middle line:90%


00:42:31.640 --> 00:42:36.920 align:middle line:84%
So now we have this weird thing
where you're feeding in h,

00:42:36.920 --> 00:42:41.450 align:middle line:84%
and then you can use a degree
like h polynomial to convert it

00:42:41.450 --> 00:42:42.900 align:middle line:90%
to 0, 1s.

00:42:42.900 --> 00:42:45.320 align:middle line:84%
And then now you can feed it
into this Boolean formula.

00:42:45.320 --> 00:42:48.620 align:middle line:84%
And for this part, we're
going to just arithmetize it.

00:42:48.620 --> 00:42:51.170 align:middle line:84%
So with arithmetizing--
just to recall.

00:42:51.170 --> 00:42:58.190 align:middle line:84%
So arithmetizing is you
can take an and gate

00:42:58.190 --> 00:43:01.310 align:middle line:84%
and replace it with the
polynomial-- a plus b

00:43:01.310 --> 00:43:03.530 align:middle line:90%
minus a times b.

00:43:03.530 --> 00:43:07.340 align:middle line:84%
And then-- sorry, this
is-- should be in, not add.

00:43:07.340 --> 00:43:11.150 align:middle line:90%
And then OR of a, b--

00:43:11.150 --> 00:43:12.000 align:middle line:90%
is this right?

00:43:12.000 --> 00:43:12.500 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:43:12.500 --> 00:43:14.175 align:middle line:90%
It's the way.

00:43:14.175 --> 00:43:19.110 align:middle line:84%
This is a plus b minus a,
b, and this is a times b.

00:43:19.110 --> 00:43:20.920 align:middle line:84%
So every time there's
a OR gate here,

00:43:20.920 --> 00:43:23.410 align:middle line:84%
you can replace it
by this polynomial.

00:43:23.410 --> 00:43:26.118 align:middle line:84%
And if it's an AND gate, you can
replace it with this polynomial.

00:43:26.118 --> 00:43:30.230 align:middle line:90%


00:43:30.230 --> 00:43:32.192 align:middle line:84%
So maybe-- there's
another lemma that Yael

00:43:32.192 --> 00:43:34.150 align:middle line:84%
proved during the first
class that-- maybe it's

00:43:34.150 --> 00:43:35.817 align:middle line:84%
been too long to
remember, but maybe you

00:43:35.817 --> 00:43:37.060 align:middle line:90%
can try to rethink about it.

00:43:37.060 --> 00:43:39.940 align:middle line:84%
So the question is,
what is the degree

00:43:39.940 --> 00:43:43.220 align:middle line:84%
of the final polynomial you get
here when you arithmetize it?

00:43:43.220 --> 00:43:58.308 align:middle line:90%


00:43:58.308 --> 00:43:59.600 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Is it 2 to the depth?

00:43:59.600 --> 00:44:01.243 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]

00:44:01.243 --> 00:44:02.660 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah,
yeah, exactly.

00:44:02.660 --> 00:44:04.270 align:middle line:84%
So it's a number of
input gates here.

00:44:04.270 --> 00:44:06.520 align:middle line:84%
I think that's the way she
said it in the first class.

00:44:06.520 --> 00:44:08.440 align:middle line:84%
It was just a number
of input gates, times--

00:44:08.440 --> 00:44:10.315 align:middle line:84%
I guess, here, you had
to times by the degree

00:44:10.315 --> 00:44:13.390 align:middle line:84%
of each thing you're feeding
in, which is to be h minus 1.

00:44:13.390 --> 00:44:17.130 align:middle line:90%
So it's going to be--

00:44:17.130 --> 00:44:21.378 align:middle line:84%
log S prime, which is the number
of gates in the bottom, times--

00:44:21.378 --> 00:44:22.920 align:middle line:84%
let's just say h
instead of h minus 1

00:44:22.920 --> 00:44:24.170 align:middle line:90%
because it's a little shorter.

00:44:24.170 --> 00:44:26.990 align:middle line:90%


00:44:26.990 --> 00:44:29.390 align:middle line:84%
So this is going to
be the polynomial

00:44:29.390 --> 00:44:33.590 align:middle line:84%
that we're going to use for
add tilde and mult tilde.

00:44:33.590 --> 00:44:38.100 align:middle line:84%
So it's not the low-degree
extension but it's a good thing.

00:44:38.100 --> 00:44:38.600 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:44:38.600 --> 00:44:39.740 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Is this is over--

00:44:39.740 --> 00:44:40.700 align:middle line:90%
just to be clear.

00:44:40.700 --> 00:44:42.090 align:middle line:90%
The extension is over--

00:44:42.090 --> 00:44:44.450 align:middle line:84%
is that right, or is
this over [INAUDIBLE]?

00:44:44.450 --> 00:44:46.408 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, so
I guess what's happening

00:44:46.408 --> 00:44:50.130 align:middle line:84%
is you're first
converting your h over--

00:44:50.130 --> 00:44:50.640 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

00:44:50.640 --> 00:44:51.510 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:44:51.510 --> 00:44:52.113 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:44:52.113 --> 00:44:53.530 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The way
you've drawn it,

00:44:53.530 --> 00:44:56.880 align:middle line:84%
it makes it seem like you're
converting h into log h bits.

00:44:56.880 --> 00:44:59.167 align:middle line:84%
What if I just want to
convert it to a single?

00:44:59.167 --> 00:45:00.750 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: So h
is big. h is, like,

00:45:00.750 --> 00:45:03.580 align:middle line:84%
2 to the log h sort
of information.

00:45:03.580 --> 00:45:07.410 align:middle line:84%
Or-- maybe it's the
wrong information.

00:45:07.410 --> 00:45:10.120 align:middle line:84%
So h comes from
this bigger set H.

00:45:10.120 --> 00:45:11.620 align:middle line:84%
So you want to
convert it to 0, 1 so

00:45:11.620 --> 00:45:13.328 align:middle line:84%
you can feed it into
the Boolean formula.

00:45:13.328 --> 00:45:14.950 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, it's not
type-compatible.

00:45:14.950 --> 00:45:19.038 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But the Boolean
formula only takes in--

00:45:19.038 --> 00:45:20.580 align:middle line:84%
like, when you fix
a Boolean formula,

00:45:20.580 --> 00:45:23.000 align:middle line:84%
it has a bunch of
inputs that are bits.

00:45:23.000 --> 00:45:27.830 align:middle line:84%
But each bit-- you
called them [INAUDIBLE].

00:45:27.830 --> 00:45:31.940 align:middle line:84%
So how do you plug in
log h bits into an input

00:45:31.940 --> 00:45:33.112 align:middle line:90%
that only have one bit?

00:45:33.112 --> 00:45:34.070 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: OK, Yeah.

00:45:34.070 --> 00:45:37.460 align:middle line:84%
So you can convert this
into a number of bits.

00:45:37.460 --> 00:45:41.510 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be
log h many bits.

00:45:41.510 --> 00:45:44.630 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The first
log n [INAUDIBLE].

00:45:44.630 --> 00:45:46.647 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:45:46.647 --> 00:45:50.778 align:middle line:90%


00:45:50.778 --> 00:45:52.320 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah,
so the conversion

00:45:52.320 --> 00:45:54.610 align:middle line:84%
isn't from one h
symbol to one bit.

00:45:54.610 --> 00:45:56.920 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be one
h symbol to many bits.

00:45:56.920 --> 00:45:57.917 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: To many bits.

00:45:57.917 --> 00:45:58.750 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Right.

00:45:58.750 --> 00:46:01.793 align:middle line:90%
Many is going to 1 h bits.

00:46:01.793 --> 00:46:03.210 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I thought
we just wanted

00:46:03.210 --> 00:46:06.112 align:middle line:84%
to find a big
argument in circuits

00:46:06.112 --> 00:46:10.247 align:middle line:84%
is that when you evaluate it,
when the h and r is 0 and 1,

00:46:10.247 --> 00:46:11.580 align:middle line:90%
it's equivalent to the original.

00:46:11.580 --> 00:46:11.730 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: OK.

00:46:11.730 --> 00:46:12.190 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, sure.

00:46:12.190 --> 00:46:13.857 align:middle line:84%
So if you want to
keep this-- this in 0,

00:46:13.857 --> 00:46:16.170 align:middle line:84%
1 so you can have big
set here H just be 0, 1,

00:46:16.170 --> 00:46:17.530 align:middle line:90%
then this is just identity.

00:46:17.530 --> 00:46:18.990 align:middle line:84%
You can just plug
it straight in.

00:46:18.990 --> 00:46:20.640 align:middle line:90%
I guess this conversion is--

00:46:20.640 --> 00:46:22.410 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:46:22.410 --> 00:46:26.070 align:middle line:84%
Yael called the thing based on
whether H is like a bigger set.

00:46:26.070 --> 00:46:27.570 align:middle line:84%
So I've just been
converting it to H

00:46:27.570 --> 00:46:29.160 align:middle line:90%
is pretty much that, basically.

00:46:29.160 --> 00:46:31.620 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Oh, OK h
is different than f.

00:46:31.620 --> 00:46:33.248 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

00:46:33.248 --> 00:46:33.790 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:46:33.790 --> 00:46:41.258 align:middle line:90%


00:46:41.258 --> 00:46:43.800 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So you're literally
just saying arithmetize, right?

00:46:43.800 --> 00:46:46.290 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: I'm literally
just saying arithmetize, yeah.

00:46:46.290 --> 00:46:49.710 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And it's still
a deterministic procedure.

00:46:49.710 --> 00:46:51.675 align:middle line:84%
So you'll get to
some unique point,

00:46:51.675 --> 00:46:53.050 align:middle line:84%
given the same
[INAUDIBLE] point,

00:46:53.050 --> 00:46:54.842 align:middle line:84%
but it's just not the
low-degree extension.

00:46:54.842 --> 00:46:56.430 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

00:46:56.430 --> 00:46:58.590 align:middle line:84%
But it's low enough
degree, which

00:46:58.590 --> 00:47:01.182 align:middle line:84%
is going to be the number
here, because if it

00:47:01.182 --> 00:47:04.090 align:middle line:84%
was a really low degree,
it would just be h degree.

00:47:04.090 --> 00:47:06.400 align:middle line:90%
But this is a log factor bigger.

00:47:06.400 --> 00:47:10.380 align:middle line:84%
But that's going to be OK for us
because, as a verifier, that's

00:47:10.380 --> 00:47:10.990 align:middle line:90%
fine, I guess.

00:47:10.990 --> 00:47:11.490 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:47:11.490 --> 00:47:13.805 align:middle line:90%


00:47:13.805 --> 00:47:16.080 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I'm still a
little bit confused about--

00:47:16.080 --> 00:47:23.310 align:middle line:84%
so b add basically computes
for one gate, right?

00:47:23.310 --> 00:47:26.670 align:middle line:84%
That essentially like
computes-- given one gate

00:47:26.670 --> 00:47:29.430 align:middle line:84%
it simulates the circuit for
one particular gate, right?

00:47:29.430 --> 00:47:30.210 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: No.

00:47:30.210 --> 00:47:34.710 align:middle line:84%
So this formula-- so the input
is going to be any particular

00:47:34.710 --> 00:47:37.710 align:middle line:84%
location in your entire circuit,
and it's going to compute this

00:47:37.710 --> 00:47:38.820 align:middle line:90%
log depth--

00:47:38.820 --> 00:47:41.460 align:middle line:84%
this polylog-size formula to
tell you what that gate should

00:47:41.460 --> 00:47:42.288 align:middle line:90%
be--

00:47:42.288 --> 00:47:43.830 align:middle line:84%
whether that was
the add gate or not.

00:47:43.830 --> 00:47:45.520 align:middle line:84%
So the input is not
the specific gate.

00:47:45.520 --> 00:47:49.830 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be any of the
gates in the entire circuit.

00:47:49.830 --> 00:47:54.320 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, so it's one
formula for the entire circuit.

00:47:54.320 --> 00:47:56.612 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, one
formula for the entire circuit.

00:47:56.612 --> 00:47:58.260 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: But then why are you--

00:47:58.260 --> 00:48:01.210 align:middle line:90%


00:48:01.210 --> 00:48:02.930 align:middle line:90%
and so where exactly--

00:48:02.930 --> 00:48:05.330 align:middle line:90%
what-- we have these h's.

00:48:05.330 --> 00:48:07.590 align:middle line:84%
And that means that we
have, technically, I

00:48:07.590 --> 00:48:08.990 align:middle line:90%
guess the same number of inputs.

00:48:08.990 --> 00:48:11.960 align:middle line:84%
But we have blocks
of inputs now.

00:48:11.960 --> 00:48:14.120 align:middle line:84%
And how exactly
does that change?

00:48:14.120 --> 00:48:16.960 align:middle line:84%
Because that means
that we have--

00:48:16.960 --> 00:48:25.300 align:middle line:84%
technically, we're combining--
it just feels weird

00:48:25.300 --> 00:48:29.140 align:middle line:84%
because, in some sense, it feels
like we're mixing up information

00:48:29.140 --> 00:48:30.340 align:middle line:90%
because we're saying--

00:48:30.340 --> 00:48:34.060 align:middle line:90%
we're assigning one thing--

00:48:34.060 --> 00:48:38.950 align:middle line:84%
one thing in a large space to a
bunch of things in the Boolean

00:48:38.950 --> 00:48:41.410 align:middle line:90%
space, of the [INAUDIBLE].

00:48:41.410 --> 00:48:44.050 align:middle line:84%
In the large field-- h is
in the larger field, right?

00:48:44.050 --> 00:48:46.390 align:middle line:84%
And we're saying that
we're just assigning

00:48:46.390 --> 00:48:49.678 align:middle line:84%
one h to this spread of things
in the [INAUDIBLE] field.

00:48:49.678 --> 00:48:50.470 align:middle line:90%
And it just feels--

00:48:50.470 --> 00:48:53.290 align:middle line:90%


00:48:53.290 --> 00:48:55.480 align:middle line:84%
I'm not [INAUDIBLE] a
lot [? of things. ?]

00:48:55.480 --> 00:48:56.510 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: OK, yeah.

00:48:56.510 --> 00:48:59.123 align:middle line:84%
So maybe the confusion is,
like, this part is 0, 1,

00:48:59.123 --> 00:49:00.290 align:middle line:90%
so it's really just Boolean.

00:49:00.290 --> 00:49:02.207 align:middle line:84%
And then over here, we
have this weird h going

00:49:02.207 --> 00:49:04.095 align:middle line:90%
into it, which is not Boolean.

00:49:04.095 --> 00:49:05.470 align:middle line:84%
So, what is this
polynomial doing

00:49:05.470 --> 00:49:06.680 align:middle line:90%
with all this Boolean stuff?

00:49:06.680 --> 00:49:07.972 align:middle line:90%
So that's part of the question?

00:49:07.972 --> 00:49:10.010 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so maybe we should
think about it as in--

00:49:10.010 --> 00:49:12.562 align:middle line:84%
Let's first arithmetize
this circuit here.

00:49:12.562 --> 00:49:15.020 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to get something
over a large field already.

00:49:15.020 --> 00:49:17.350 align:middle line:84%
And now we're going to
plug in these polynomials

00:49:17.350 --> 00:49:19.270 align:middle line:84%
into the arithmetized
thing, which is already

00:49:19.270 --> 00:49:20.228 align:middle line:90%
going to be polynomial.

00:49:20.228 --> 00:49:24.218 align:middle line:84%
So we're-- it's like
polynomial composition.

00:49:24.218 --> 00:49:26.510 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But the thing is
you're not doing one to one.

00:49:26.510 --> 00:49:27.967 align:middle line:90%
You're doing one to many.

00:49:27.967 --> 00:49:30.550 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: If you think about
it, the amount of information

00:49:30.550 --> 00:49:33.140 align:middle line:84%
is conserved because
here, there's H options--

00:49:33.140 --> 00:49:35.150 align:middle line:90%
capital H options-- for this h1.

00:49:35.150 --> 00:49:36.800 align:middle line:84%
And then when you
plug it up here,

00:49:36.800 --> 00:49:38.860 align:middle line:84%
you now still have
only h options

00:49:38.860 --> 00:49:43.140 align:middle line:90%
for the resulting 0, 1 string.

00:49:43.140 --> 00:49:46.590 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well-- I
mean, yes, but it also--

00:49:46.590 --> 00:49:52.920 align:middle line:84%
it felt that-- yes, but in the
higher field, we only have--

00:49:52.920 --> 00:49:57.840 align:middle line:84%
now we only have the log
S prime different inputs

00:49:57.840 --> 00:49:59.230 align:middle line:90%
from the larger field.

00:49:59.230 --> 00:50:03.466 align:middle line:84%
Now we're jamming
them into this--

00:50:03.466 --> 00:50:06.810 align:middle line:90%
or rather, we only have a few--

00:50:06.810 --> 00:50:10.050 align:middle line:90%
we have much less things--

00:50:10.050 --> 00:50:13.170 align:middle line:84%
the input from the larger field,
and we're jamming that in.

00:50:13.170 --> 00:50:18.190 align:middle line:84%
And it just-- each
element is getting

00:50:18.190 --> 00:50:21.400 align:middle line:84%
is covering a bunch of bits,
and those might [INAUDIBLE].

00:50:21.400 --> 00:50:23.900 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: So it turns out
that there is a unique mapping

00:50:23.900 --> 00:50:27.720 align:middle line:90%
from the string of h1 up to hm.

00:50:27.720 --> 00:50:31.520 align:middle line:84%
And then the 0, 1 to
the log S prime string.

00:50:31.520 --> 00:50:32.820 align:middle line:90%
And so it's a bijection.

00:50:32.820 --> 00:50:34.445 align:middle line:84%
You can go back and
forth between them.

00:50:34.445 --> 00:50:36.420 align:middle line:84%
So it's actually--
there's no clashing.

00:50:36.420 --> 00:50:38.860 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be
just one to one.

00:50:38.860 --> 00:50:39.520 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:50:39.520 --> 00:50:40.430 align:middle line:90%
I'm sorry.

00:50:40.430 --> 00:50:42.350 align:middle line:84%
I'm just not following
the bijection.

00:50:42.350 --> 00:50:43.090 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]

00:50:43.090 --> 00:50:44.500 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Sure, OK.

00:50:44.500 --> 00:50:45.110 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:50:45.110 --> 00:50:47.060 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I guess that's
where my [INAUDIBLE].

00:50:47.060 --> 00:50:47.852 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Sure.

00:50:47.852 --> 00:50:50.420 align:middle line:84%
OK, so what's going
to happen is--

00:50:50.420 --> 00:50:51.670 align:middle line:90%
maybe let me zoom out of that.

00:50:51.670 --> 00:50:52.503 align:middle line:90%
It's a little messy.

00:50:52.503 --> 00:50:55.812 align:middle line:84%
So let's say you have--
this is going to be just--

00:50:55.812 --> 00:50:58.020 align:middle line:84%
because this is going to be
our 0, 1 layer over here.

00:50:58.020 --> 00:50:58.880 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I'm sorry again for--

00:50:58.880 --> 00:50:59.810 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah,
no, you're fine.

00:50:59.810 --> 00:51:00.990 align:middle line:90%
It's good to ask questions.

00:51:00.990 --> 00:51:05.450 align:middle line:84%
So this is going to be
log h number of bits.

00:51:05.450 --> 00:51:09.350 align:middle line:84%
And the idea is we're
going to take some h,

00:51:09.350 --> 00:51:15.600 align:middle line:84%
and we're going to map it here
to log h many different bits.

00:51:15.600 --> 00:51:17.810 align:middle line:84%
So here-- one way
you can do this--

00:51:17.810 --> 00:51:19.890 align:middle line:84%
and you can write
this in binary.

00:51:19.890 --> 00:51:22.560 align:middle line:84%
So maybe this-- maybe h
here is, like, size 8.

00:51:22.560 --> 00:51:24.750 align:middle line:84%
So this would correspond
to a binary string.

00:51:24.750 --> 00:51:26.240 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I get you can do that.

00:51:26.240 --> 00:51:29.550 align:middle line:84%
But I'm like, why are
we allowed to do that?

00:51:29.550 --> 00:51:32.840 align:middle line:84%
And how exactly-- why
exactly is this helping us?

00:51:32.840 --> 00:51:36.530 align:middle line:84%
I understand that you can write
it in binary on only that.

00:51:36.530 --> 00:51:38.850 align:middle line:84%
How exactly is this
helping us again?

00:51:38.850 --> 00:51:42.900 align:middle line:84%
Because it feels like
now each element--

00:51:42.900 --> 00:51:45.650 align:middle line:84%
each big element in the
extension-- like, before,

00:51:45.650 --> 00:51:49.370 align:middle line:84%
we were just saying that
we're going to extend each

00:51:49.370 --> 00:51:50.990 align:middle line:90%
individual--

00:51:50.990 --> 00:51:53.420 align:middle line:84%
we're going to assume
that 0, 1 values

00:51:53.420 --> 00:51:55.810 align:middle line:84%
are actually now
extended values.

00:51:55.810 --> 00:51:59.265 align:middle line:84%
But then, now, we're doing
it in such a way that--

00:51:59.265 --> 00:52:02.310 align:middle line:84%
because before, we were
assuming these values

00:52:02.310 --> 00:52:04.260 align:middle line:90%
from the large circuit are--

00:52:04.260 --> 00:52:08.388 align:middle line:90%


00:52:08.388 --> 00:52:10.950 align:middle line:90%
we're going to say--

00:52:10.950 --> 00:52:14.400 align:middle line:84%
if happen to have, obviously,
an AND gate, then we're

00:52:14.400 --> 00:52:17.340 align:middle line:84%
going to have one big value
and another big value,

00:52:17.340 --> 00:52:19.350 align:middle line:84%
and then multiply them
together [INAUDIBLE].

00:52:19.350 --> 00:52:19.785 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: OK.

00:52:19.785 --> 00:52:20.220 align:middle line:90%
Can you hold that?

00:52:20.220 --> 00:52:21.210 align:middle line:84%
How about if we
take a break now,

00:52:21.210 --> 00:52:22.500 align:middle line:84%
and then we'll come
back in five minutes,

00:52:22.500 --> 00:52:23.740 align:middle line:84%
and then we'll do the
second half of the class?

00:52:23.740 --> 00:52:25.030 align:middle line:84%
I think that's
all for this part.

00:52:25.030 --> 00:52:26.110 align:middle line:90%
And maybe we can talk offline.

00:52:26.110 --> 00:52:26.530 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Sorry.

00:52:26.530 --> 00:52:28.113 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: No,
you're totally fine.

00:52:28.113 --> 00:52:29.610 align:middle line:90%
Let's talk about it then.

00:52:29.610 --> 00:52:30.690 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:52:30.690 --> 00:52:34.710 align:middle line:84%
So how many of you guys
know the theorem exists

00:52:34.710 --> 00:52:37.950 align:middle line:90%
or what it says or anything?

00:52:37.950 --> 00:52:39.450 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Somehow,
I already forgot.

00:52:39.450 --> 00:52:40.380 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: OK, it's fine.

00:52:40.380 --> 00:52:41.200 align:middle line:90%
We'll talk about it again.

00:52:41.200 --> 00:52:42.640 align:middle line:84%
So this is a really
famous theorem.

00:52:42.640 --> 00:52:43.515 align:middle line:90%
It's a great theorem.

00:52:43.515 --> 00:52:45.060 align:middle line:90%
It's very good.

00:52:45.060 --> 00:52:47.140 align:middle line:84%
So we should talk about
what these things are.

00:52:47.140 --> 00:52:50.370 align:middle line:84%
So can one of the local
complexity theorists people

00:52:50.370 --> 00:52:51.245 align:middle line:90%
tell you what IP is?

00:52:51.245 --> 00:52:53.763 align:middle line:90%


00:52:53.763 --> 00:52:55.930 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't have to be the
local complexity theorist.

00:52:55.930 --> 00:52:57.374 align:middle line:90%
It could be any of you.

00:52:57.374 --> 00:52:59.310 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHS]

00:52:59.310 --> 00:53:01.810 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Positive
statements about [INAUDIBLE]

00:53:01.810 --> 00:53:04.935 align:middle line:84%
the interactive protocol between
polytime [INAUDIBLE] verifier

00:53:04.935 --> 00:53:06.560 align:middle line:84%
and the
computationally-bounded prover.

00:53:06.560 --> 00:53:09.628 align:middle line:90%


00:53:09.628 --> 00:53:10.920 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Unbounded prover.

00:53:10.920 --> 00:53:13.780 align:middle line:90%


00:53:13.780 --> 00:53:15.130 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:53:15.130 --> 00:53:21.410 align:middle line:90%


00:53:21.410 --> 00:53:22.020 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:53:22.020 --> 00:53:38.130 align:middle line:90%


00:53:38.130 --> 00:53:38.630 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:53:38.630 --> 00:53:39.130 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:53:39.130 --> 00:53:41.330 align:middle line:84%
So-- OK, so IP is a class
of all statements that

00:53:41.330 --> 00:53:43.400 align:middle line:90%
have interactive proofs.

00:53:43.400 --> 00:53:44.200 align:middle line:90%
Interactive.

00:53:44.200 --> 00:53:44.800 align:middle line:90%
That's right.

00:53:44.800 --> 00:53:45.410 align:middle line:90%
OK, yeah.

00:53:45.410 --> 00:53:47.930 align:middle line:84%
And the prover can
be unbounded time.

00:53:47.930 --> 00:53:49.937 align:middle line:84%
And then the verifier's
running on poly time.

00:53:49.937 --> 00:53:52.020 align:middle line:84%
So the interactive proof
should also be poly size.

00:53:52.020 --> 00:53:53.950 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, the
verifier can't read it.

00:53:53.950 --> 00:53:54.950 align:middle line:90%
And then what is PSPACE?

00:53:54.950 --> 00:54:03.190 align:middle line:90%


00:54:03.190 --> 00:54:05.500 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Polynomial
space-bounded TM languages?

00:54:05.500 --> 00:54:06.333 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Great.

00:54:06.333 --> 00:54:06.910 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:54:06.910 --> 00:54:09.350 align:middle line:90%
Polyspace computations.

00:54:09.350 --> 00:54:09.850 align:middle line:90%
Yay.

00:54:09.850 --> 00:54:15.660 align:middle line:90%


00:54:15.660 --> 00:54:18.410 align:middle line:90%
And so the easier direction--

00:54:18.410 --> 00:54:19.600 align:middle line:90%
so it was shown in 1992.

00:54:19.600 --> 00:54:22.030 align:middle line:84%
These two things
are actually equal.

00:54:22.030 --> 00:54:26.050 align:middle line:84%
So the easier direction to
show is that IP is in PSPACE.

00:54:26.050 --> 00:54:28.120 align:middle line:84%
The way you do that
is by simulating

00:54:28.120 --> 00:54:29.680 align:middle line:84%
all possible
transcripts, and then

00:54:29.680 --> 00:54:31.360 align:middle line:90%
you can do some counting stuff.

00:54:31.360 --> 00:54:32.950 align:middle line:90%
We won't go into the details.

00:54:32.950 --> 00:54:35.950 align:middle line:84%
But in 1992, it was shown the
other direction, also held.

00:54:35.950 --> 00:54:40.980 align:middle line:90%
So this is by Shamir in 1992.

00:54:40.980 --> 00:54:44.230 align:middle line:84%
So in fact, PSPACE
is also in IP,

00:54:44.230 --> 00:54:48.900 align:middle line:84%
which means that these two
classes are actually equal.

00:54:48.900 --> 00:54:52.860 align:middle line:84%
And to do this, he constructed
an interactive proof

00:54:52.860 --> 00:54:55.950 align:middle line:84%
for any PSPACE
computation, and that

00:54:55.950 --> 00:54:58.890 align:middle line:90%
would give you the inclusion.

00:54:58.890 --> 00:55:08.940 align:middle line:84%
So in particular, this means
that any PSPACE computation

00:55:08.940 --> 00:55:10.040 align:middle line:90%
has interactive proof.

00:55:10.040 --> 00:55:14.780 align:middle line:90%


00:55:14.780 --> 00:55:16.817 align:middle line:84%
So going back to the
concept of delegation,

00:55:16.817 --> 00:55:18.650 align:middle line:84%
which is what this class
is entirely about--

00:55:18.650 --> 00:55:20.930 align:middle line:84%
how do you delegate
a computation

00:55:20.930 --> 00:55:23.820 align:middle line:84%
to a bigger prover, one
you can run very quickly?

00:55:23.820 --> 00:55:25.880 align:middle line:90%
Well, this is delegation, right?

00:55:25.880 --> 00:55:27.480 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:55:27.480 --> 00:55:30.290 align:middle line:84%
It's basically like, I want
you to compute some small space

00:55:30.290 --> 00:55:32.120 align:middle line:90%
computation, like polyspace.

00:55:32.120 --> 00:55:33.680 align:middle line:84%
And I can ask the
cloud, hey, can you

00:55:33.680 --> 00:55:36.030 align:middle line:84%
run this awesome Shamir
interactive proof for me

00:55:36.030 --> 00:55:39.065 align:middle line:84%
and just tell me-- prove to
me that you did it correctly?

00:55:39.065 --> 00:55:41.870 align:middle line:90%
And we're good, right?

00:55:41.870 --> 00:55:43.080 align:middle line:90%
So what's the issue?

00:55:43.080 --> 00:55:45.140 align:middle line:90%
Why aren't we done?

00:55:45.140 --> 00:55:46.970 align:middle line:90%
Why did we do GKR at all?

00:55:46.970 --> 00:55:53.040 align:middle line:84%
The reason is because,
here, the prover runtime

00:55:53.040 --> 00:55:54.065 align:middle line:90%
is 2 to the S squared.

00:55:54.065 --> 00:55:57.930 align:middle line:90%


00:55:57.930 --> 00:56:01.170 align:middle line:84%
So in PSPACE-- any computation
is doable in time 2 to the S.

00:56:01.170 --> 00:56:05.850 align:middle line:84%
But here's this additional--
so this is S2 that's squared.

00:56:05.850 --> 00:56:08.310 align:middle line:84%
So basically, the time to prove
is much more than the time

00:56:08.310 --> 00:56:10.303 align:middle line:84%
necessary to simply
compute, which

00:56:10.303 --> 00:56:11.970 align:middle line:84%
is why there's all
these follow-up works

00:56:11.970 --> 00:56:15.630 align:middle line:84%
and why this is actually so--
an interesting question today.

00:56:15.630 --> 00:56:17.230 align:middle line:84%
And there are some
follow-up work.

00:56:17.230 --> 00:56:19.390 align:middle line:84%
I don't know the reference,
and Yael didn't either.

00:56:19.390 --> 00:56:20.980 align:middle line:84%
And I don't know if
memory is wrong either.

00:56:20.980 --> 00:56:21.640 align:middle line:90%
So it could be wrong.

00:56:21.640 --> 00:56:22.360 align:middle line:90%
It could be true.

00:56:22.360 --> 00:56:23.920 align:middle line:90%
So it's a possible statement.

00:56:23.920 --> 00:56:26.790 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:56:26.790 --> 00:56:29.010 align:middle line:84%
So it's possible that
there's a follow-up work that

00:56:29.010 --> 00:56:32.400 align:middle line:84%
showed how to have a proved
runtime of 2 to the S log S,

00:56:32.400 --> 00:56:34.900 align:middle line:84%
which is significantly better
than 2 to the S squared.

00:56:34.900 --> 00:56:36.990 align:middle line:84%
But it's still not
2 to the S, which

00:56:36.990 --> 00:56:39.810 align:middle line:84%
would be the best you could
hope for any arbitrary PSPACE

00:56:39.810 --> 00:56:41.550 align:middle line:90%
computation.

00:56:41.550 --> 00:56:43.595 align:middle line:84%
So today, what we're
going to show is--

00:56:43.595 --> 00:56:47.280 align:middle line:90%


00:56:47.280 --> 00:56:50.390 align:middle line:84%
so today, the theorem is
going to be as follows.

00:56:50.390 --> 00:56:52.880 align:middle line:84%
So assuming GKR, which is
an unconditional statement.

00:56:52.880 --> 00:56:53.520 align:middle line:90%
It's not an assumption.

00:56:53.520 --> 00:56:55.895 align:middle line:84%
It's just-- this is what we're
going to use in the proof.

00:56:55.895 --> 00:57:06.620 align:middle line:84%
So since we have GKR, we can
get IP equals PSPACE with--

00:57:06.620 --> 00:57:09.380 align:middle line:90%
time 2 to the--

00:57:09.380 --> 00:57:14.555 align:middle line:84%
I should probably right
poly 2 to the S prover.

00:57:14.555 --> 00:57:21.780 align:middle line:90%


00:57:21.780 --> 00:57:23.480 align:middle line:84%
So what this means
is-- so for, maybe,

00:57:23.480 --> 00:57:26.470 align:middle line:84%
the hardest PSPACE computations,
assuming that, I guess,

00:57:26.470 --> 00:57:28.470 align:middle line:84%
the hardest thing you can
do is time 2 to the S,

00:57:28.470 --> 00:57:31.580 align:middle line:84%
then we do have actually
efficient delegation protocols

00:57:31.580 --> 00:57:32.460 align:middle line:90%
for these things.

00:57:32.460 --> 00:57:34.168 align:middle line:84%
And they're, in fact,
also proof systems.

00:57:34.168 --> 00:57:38.510 align:middle line:84%
So they are, like, information
theoretically secure.

00:57:38.510 --> 00:57:41.510 align:middle line:84%
And maybe I'll just mention
one open question is, what

00:57:41.510 --> 00:57:44.240 align:middle line:90%
about general time TSPACE--

00:57:44.240 --> 00:57:45.000 align:middle line:90%
space computation?

00:57:45.000 --> 00:57:51.570 align:middle line:84%
So-- where T's
not necessarily S,

00:57:51.570 --> 00:57:58.169 align:middle line:84%
can you do an interactive
proof as efficient?

00:57:58.169 --> 00:58:03.330 align:middle line:90%


00:58:03.330 --> 00:58:05.460 align:middle line:90%
I think this is not known.

00:58:05.460 --> 00:58:07.655 align:middle line:84%
So if you want to think
about some proof systems,

00:58:07.655 --> 00:58:09.280 align:middle line:84%
this is a question
you can think about.

00:58:09.280 --> 00:58:15.830 align:middle line:90%


00:58:15.830 --> 00:58:17.370 align:middle line:90%
Are you guys OK if I raise this?

00:58:17.370 --> 00:58:18.160 align:middle line:90%
This is probably fine, right?

00:58:18.160 --> 00:58:18.350 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:58:18.350 --> 00:58:19.940 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Here, efficient
is, like, poly T?

00:58:19.940 --> 00:58:22.190 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, poly T.
And you can definitely ask

00:58:22.190 --> 00:58:23.730 align:middle line:90%
more-- even stronger questions.

00:58:23.730 --> 00:58:27.120 align:middle line:84%
Can you do it with linear time
overhead, additive, whatever,

00:58:27.120 --> 00:58:29.600 align:middle line:90%
overhead?

00:58:29.600 --> 00:58:31.590 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, there's many questions.

00:58:31.590 --> 00:58:51.390 align:middle line:90%


00:58:51.390 --> 00:58:55.500 align:middle line:84%
OK, so how do we describe
a PSPACE computation?

00:58:55.500 --> 00:58:58.080 align:middle line:84%
Someone said that it's a
space of all computations that

00:58:58.080 --> 00:59:00.840 align:middle line:84%
are doable by a Turing
machine with bounded space.

00:59:00.840 --> 00:59:02.340 align:middle line:84%
So let's say that
we have our Turing

00:59:02.340 --> 00:59:13.530 align:middle line:84%
machine, M. And let's
say it runs in space, S.

00:59:13.530 --> 00:59:20.390 align:middle line:84%
And maybe I could say,
on input 0, 1 to the n.

00:59:20.390 --> 00:59:24.545 align:middle line:90%
And maybe the thing we want is--

00:59:24.545 --> 00:59:27.200 align:middle line:90%


00:59:27.200 --> 00:59:28.940 align:middle line:90%
and maybe we can just say time--

00:59:28.940 --> 00:59:32.900 align:middle line:84%
and also time, T. And I guess
I could set it to 2 to the S,

00:59:32.900 --> 00:59:33.560 align:middle line:90%
just for now.

00:59:33.560 --> 00:59:34.140 align:middle line:90%
Is that OK?

00:59:34.140 --> 00:59:36.140 align:middle line:84%
So let's say that maybe
we have a Turing machine

00:59:36.140 --> 00:59:38.900 align:middle line:84%
and we want to see that within
time T, which is 2 to the S,

00:59:38.900 --> 00:59:41.060 align:middle line:84%
that eventually, it
will get to the output,

00:59:41.060 --> 00:59:43.195 align:middle line:84%
like the accepting
configuration, or not.

00:59:43.195 --> 00:59:45.920 align:middle line:90%


00:59:45.920 --> 00:59:55.930 align:middle line:84%
So by this point, it should
get to accepting configuration.

00:59:55.930 --> 01:00:05.220 align:middle line:90%


01:00:05.220 --> 01:00:06.830 align:middle line:90%
So here's a trick.

01:00:06.830 --> 01:00:09.770 align:middle line:84%
The idea here, we're going to
use GKR to prove the theorem.

01:00:09.770 --> 01:00:11.970 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to have
to construct some circuit

01:00:11.970 --> 01:00:14.780 align:middle line:84%
to delegate some
computation with GKR with.

01:00:14.780 --> 01:00:17.430 align:middle line:90%


01:00:17.430 --> 01:00:21.150 align:middle line:84%
So this Turing machine here-- it
runs in space S-- can run time--

01:00:21.150 --> 01:00:24.407 align:middle line:84%
up to 2 to the S. So
if you naively do this,

01:00:24.407 --> 01:00:26.490 align:middle line:84%
you would be [INAUDIBLE]
depth 2 to the S circuit.

01:00:26.490 --> 01:00:27.460 align:middle line:90%
That's not so good.

01:00:27.460 --> 01:00:31.870 align:middle line:84%
We want it, really,
to be depth S.

01:00:31.870 --> 01:00:33.620 align:middle line:84%
So the thing about let
me write that down.

01:00:33.620 --> 01:00:50.720 align:middle line:84%
So the goal is to convert
to depth S. That's the goal.

01:00:50.720 --> 01:00:52.280 align:middle line:90%
So here's the trick.

01:00:52.280 --> 01:00:53.290 align:middle line:90%
The trick is as follows.

01:00:53.290 --> 01:00:54.832 align:middle line:84%
We're going to look
at the transition

01:00:54.832 --> 01:01:00.180 align:middle line:84%
matrix of all the possible
states of the Turing tape,

01:01:00.180 --> 01:01:03.095 align:middle line:90%
basically.

01:01:03.095 --> 01:01:05.670 align:middle line:84%
So we're to have a bunch of
different possible states here

01:01:05.670 --> 01:01:07.940 align:middle line:84%
and a bunch of different
possible states here.

01:01:07.940 --> 01:01:12.680 align:middle line:84%
So how many possible
states are there?

01:01:12.680 --> 01:01:17.030 align:middle line:84%
Well, there is 0, 1 to the S.
That's what the state could

01:01:17.030 --> 01:01:18.570 align:middle line:90%
actually say on the tape.

01:01:18.570 --> 01:01:21.710 align:middle line:84%
But that's also where the
Turing machine is right now.

01:01:21.710 --> 01:01:26.060 align:middle line:84%
So maybe times the
index and S should

01:01:26.060 --> 01:01:28.190 align:middle line:84%
say where the Turing
machine is right now.

01:01:28.190 --> 01:01:31.670 align:middle line:84%
And then it would do one
computation, move somewhere,

01:01:31.670 --> 01:01:34.105 align:middle line:84%
and then it'll be an adjacent
place at a different state.

01:01:34.105 --> 01:01:35.480 align:middle line:84%
And then, now,
the new state will

01:01:35.480 --> 01:01:36.980 align:middle line:84%
be like a slightly
different string,

01:01:36.980 --> 01:01:38.730 align:middle line:84%
and it'll be-- the
Turing pointer will

01:01:38.730 --> 01:01:40.280 align:middle line:90%
be at a different location.

01:01:40.280 --> 01:01:42.970 align:middle line:84%
So here is also going
to be the same states.

01:01:42.970 --> 01:01:49.850 align:middle line:90%


01:01:49.850 --> 01:01:55.460 align:middle line:84%
And the point is we're going
to put a 1 at a location

01:01:55.460 --> 01:01:58.340 align:middle line:84%
if there's a way to get from
this state here to this state

01:01:58.340 --> 01:01:59.545 align:middle line:90%
here in one time step.

01:01:59.545 --> 01:02:02.820 align:middle line:90%


01:02:02.820 --> 01:02:03.880 align:middle line:90%
And 0s everywhere else.

01:02:03.880 --> 01:02:04.380 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:02:04.380 --> 01:02:10.115 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Does this state
include the internal automaton?

01:02:10.115 --> 01:02:11.740 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yes,
that's a good point.

01:02:11.740 --> 01:02:13.115 align:middle line:84%
It's actually a
very good-- yeah.

01:02:13.115 --> 01:02:16.470 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So there should only
be, like, one 1 in each row.

01:02:16.470 --> 01:02:18.720 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, there
should be one 1 in every row

01:02:18.720 --> 01:02:21.600 align:middle line:84%
because the thing you were going
to do after one time step should

01:02:21.600 --> 01:02:24.240 align:middle line:84%
be deterministic based on
your current location--

01:02:24.240 --> 01:02:26.850 align:middle line:90%
your state based on your--

01:02:26.850 --> 01:02:28.810 align:middle line:84%
your internal state of
your Turing machine,

01:02:28.810 --> 01:02:32.220 align:middle line:84%
as well as what
you see around you.

01:02:32.220 --> 01:02:34.620 align:middle line:84%
I'm using this as just the
number of states in the Turing

01:02:34.620 --> 01:02:35.538 align:middle line:90%
machine.

01:02:35.538 --> 01:02:37.580 align:middle line:84%
Anything else I'm forgetting
within these states?

01:02:37.580 --> 01:02:38.940 align:middle line:84%
There's just a
bunch of information

01:02:38.940 --> 01:02:39.857 align:middle line:90%
that I don't remember.

01:02:39.857 --> 01:02:44.280 align:middle line:90%


01:02:44.280 --> 01:02:46.340 align:middle line:90%
So anyway-- so I guess--

01:02:46.340 --> 01:02:49.680 align:middle line:84%
the key point here is that this
matrix is actually very easy

01:02:49.680 --> 01:02:55.120 align:middle line:84%
to compute, because given maybe
a state here and a state here,

01:02:55.120 --> 01:02:59.123 align:middle line:84%
you can compare very-- in very
low depth, very efficiently.

01:02:59.123 --> 01:03:00.540 align:middle line:84%
So it's a very
uniform description

01:03:00.540 --> 01:03:03.010 align:middle line:84%
of this check, or of the
circuit that will generate,

01:03:03.010 --> 01:03:05.640 align:middle line:84%
whether or not the particular
corresponding matrix

01:03:05.640 --> 01:03:09.460 align:middle line:90%
value is a 0 or 1.

01:03:09.460 --> 01:03:12.480 align:middle line:84%
And that'll be important
for us because we want--

01:03:12.480 --> 01:03:15.760 align:middle line:84%
we want a uniform circuit,
I guess, at this point.

01:03:15.760 --> 01:03:17.245 align:middle line:90%
And this will make it uniform.

01:03:17.245 --> 01:03:18.942 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So
[INAUDIBLE] previously.

01:03:18.942 --> 01:03:19.900 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, OK.

01:03:19.900 --> 01:03:24.040 align:middle line:84%
So the matrix here is easy
to compute, is my claim.

01:03:24.040 --> 01:03:27.450 align:middle line:84%
The reason is you can take any
state here, any state here.

01:03:27.450 --> 01:03:29.580 align:middle line:90%
You can do some checks.

01:03:29.580 --> 01:03:31.558 align:middle line:84%
It's a very-- it's
a small formula.

01:03:31.558 --> 01:03:33.850 align:middle line:84%
You can just check it very
easily by knowing what M is,

01:03:33.850 --> 01:03:34.877 align:middle line:90%
by knowing what you see.

01:03:34.877 --> 01:03:36.960 align:middle line:84%
And you can tell if there's
a transition from that

01:03:36.960 --> 01:03:38.280 align:middle line:90%
to the next state.

01:03:38.280 --> 01:03:41.020 align:middle line:84%
So the matrix here is easy
to compute, is my claim.

01:03:41.020 --> 01:03:56.950 align:middle line:90%


01:03:56.950 --> 01:04:01.270 align:middle line:84%
So maybe let's call
this matrix A. So

01:04:01.270 --> 01:04:03.520 align:middle line:90%
what I just said is that Aij--

01:04:03.520 --> 01:04:06.730 align:middle line:84%
this is going to be the
ith row in the jth column--

01:04:06.730 --> 01:04:11.020 align:middle line:84%
entry of A. This is
going to be a 1 if--

01:04:11.020 --> 01:04:13.050 align:middle line:84%
so state i goes to state
j in one time step.

01:04:13.050 --> 01:04:29.910 align:middle line:90%


01:04:29.910 --> 01:04:32.160 align:middle line:90%
And so that's good.

01:04:32.160 --> 01:04:36.653 align:middle line:84%
What we really want to know
is whether i will go to j

01:04:36.653 --> 01:04:38.070 align:middle line:84%
or the starting
configuration will

01:04:38.070 --> 01:04:41.352 align:middle line:84%
go to the accepting
configuration in T time steps.

01:04:41.352 --> 01:04:42.185 align:middle line:90%
That's what we want.

01:04:42.185 --> 01:05:26.680 align:middle line:90%


01:05:26.680 --> 01:05:33.630 align:middle line:84%
So here, we have a copy
of A. Maybe let's just

01:05:33.630 --> 01:05:36.010 align:middle line:84%
do some computations just
for fun and see what happens.

01:05:36.010 --> 01:05:39.840 align:middle line:84%
So you have a copy of matrix A
here, a copy of matrix A here.

01:05:39.840 --> 01:05:42.645 align:middle line:84%
Just multiply them via
matrix multiplication.

01:05:42.645 --> 01:05:43.520 align:middle line:90%
So we have A squared.

01:05:43.520 --> 01:05:46.880 align:middle line:90%


01:05:46.880 --> 01:05:50.480 align:middle line:84%
What is the value of
the entry in the ith row

01:05:50.480 --> 01:05:52.402 align:middle line:90%
and the jth column of A squared?

01:05:52.402 --> 01:05:54.170 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:05:54.170 --> 01:05:55.320 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, so--

01:05:55.320 --> 01:05:56.737 align:middle line:84%
I guess I'll write
here, actually.

01:05:56.737 --> 01:05:59.925 align:middle line:90%


01:05:59.925 --> 01:06:04.200 align:middle line:84%
So A squared-- so the
ijth entry of A squared

01:06:04.200 --> 01:06:10.327 align:middle line:84%
is whether i goes
to j in two steps.

01:06:10.327 --> 01:06:12.660 align:middle line:84%
And maybe for those of you
who haven't seen this before,

01:06:12.660 --> 01:06:14.305 align:middle line:90%
let's just do it so you see why.

01:06:14.305 --> 01:06:18.050 align:middle line:90%


01:06:18.050 --> 01:06:22.130 align:middle line:84%
So if you want to look at the
ijth entry of this product

01:06:22.130 --> 01:06:23.990 align:middle line:84%
of these two matrices,
what you want to do

01:06:23.990 --> 01:06:25.970 align:middle line:84%
is you want to take
the ith row, look

01:06:25.970 --> 01:06:28.950 align:middle line:84%
at all the entries across
it, take the jth column,

01:06:28.950 --> 01:06:31.310 align:middle line:84%
look at all the entries
down it, and you

01:06:31.310 --> 01:06:33.200 align:middle line:84%
want to take the
pairwise-- you want to take

01:06:33.200 --> 01:06:34.110 align:middle line:90%
the product and the sum.

01:06:34.110 --> 01:06:36.568 align:middle line:84%
So you want to take this entry
times this entry, this entry

01:06:36.568 --> 01:06:38.510 align:middle line:90%
times this entry, and so on.

01:06:38.510 --> 01:06:40.040 align:middle line:84%
So what's essentially
happening is,

01:06:40.040 --> 01:06:42.020 align:middle line:84%
here, you want to
take-- you want

01:06:42.020 --> 01:06:43.730 align:middle line:84%
to look at the
entry corresponding

01:06:43.730 --> 01:06:44.870 align:middle line:90%
to going from i to k.

01:06:44.870 --> 01:06:49.250 align:middle line:84%
And then you go from
k to l and k to j.

01:06:49.250 --> 01:06:51.080 align:middle line:84%
And then you do this
for k equals 2, also.

01:06:51.080 --> 01:06:51.680 align:middle line:90%
We do it for--

01:06:51.680 --> 01:06:52.722 align:middle line:90%
llike you go from i to k.

01:06:52.722 --> 01:06:55.400 align:middle line:84%
And then you'll
do it from k to j.

01:06:55.400 --> 01:06:58.287 align:middle line:84%
And so the final entry will--
the sum of all of this--

01:06:58.287 --> 01:06:59.870 align:middle line:84%
these products--
will be 1 if and only

01:06:59.870 --> 01:07:02.330 align:middle line:84%
if there is a way to
go from i to some k

01:07:02.330 --> 01:07:05.948 align:middle line:90%
to j in two time steps.

01:07:05.948 --> 01:07:06.920 align:middle line:90%
That OK?

01:07:06.920 --> 01:07:08.270 align:middle line:90%
Or-- I don't know.

01:07:08.270 --> 01:07:08.770 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:07:08.770 --> 01:07:11.320 align:middle line:90%


01:07:11.320 --> 01:07:14.650 align:middle line:84%
So in general then, if we
want to look at the transition

01:07:14.650 --> 01:07:16.450 align:middle line:90%
matrix of having--

01:07:16.450 --> 01:07:18.320 align:middle line:90%
after going for T time steps--

01:07:18.320 --> 01:07:19.720 align:middle line:90%
you keep doing this.

01:07:19.720 --> 01:07:27.900 align:middle line:84%
And you get that the ijth
entry of A to the power

01:07:27.900 --> 01:07:29.135 align:middle line:90%
is whether i goes to j.

01:07:29.135 --> 01:07:38.270 align:middle line:90%


01:07:38.270 --> 01:07:43.740 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So here, [INAUDIBLE]
whether [INAUDIBLE] exactly T

01:07:43.740 --> 01:07:44.240 align:middle line:90%
steps?

01:07:44.240 --> 01:07:45.682 align:middle line:90%
Like, no more, no less?

01:07:45.682 --> 01:07:46.640 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, OK.

01:07:46.640 --> 01:07:48.020 align:middle line:90%
That's a good point.

01:07:48.020 --> 01:07:51.410 align:middle line:84%
So maybe we can say this should
be in exactly T time steps.

01:07:51.410 --> 01:07:53.368 align:middle line:84%
And maybe you can have
your Turing machine stay

01:07:53.368 --> 01:07:55.910 align:middle line:84%
as accepting configuration if
it's already reached it, maybe,

01:07:55.910 --> 01:07:56.740 align:middle line:90%
for instance.

01:07:56.740 --> 01:07:57.990 align:middle line:90%
That's one thing you could do.

01:07:57.990 --> 01:07:59.570 align:middle line:84%
Another way you
could do it is you--

01:07:59.570 --> 01:08:00.860 align:middle line:84%
you could change this
matrix because it's

01:08:00.860 --> 01:08:02.810 align:middle line:84%
a one-to-one diagonal
so that you can always

01:08:02.810 --> 01:08:04.100 align:middle line:90%
waste time if you want.

01:08:04.100 --> 01:08:05.760 align:middle line:84%
But whatever version
you want is fine.

01:08:05.760 --> 01:08:09.000 align:middle line:84%
Just-- maybe, let's say for
now, after exactly T time steps,

01:08:09.000 --> 01:08:10.800 align:middle line:84%
it should reach the
existing configuration.

01:08:10.800 --> 01:08:13.000 align:middle line:84%
And just have it
start accepting place.

01:08:13.000 --> 01:08:20.069 align:middle line:90%


01:08:20.069 --> 01:08:21.830 align:middle line:84%
So the point is
if we can compute

01:08:21.830 --> 01:08:24.800 align:middle line:84%
what AT of the input
configuration, comma,

01:08:24.800 --> 01:08:26.600 align:middle line:84%
the accepting
configuration is, we

01:08:26.600 --> 01:08:30.930 align:middle line:84%
know if this PSPACE computation
is accepting or not.

01:08:30.930 --> 01:08:31.930 align:middle line:90%
That good?

01:08:31.930 --> 01:08:36.628 align:middle line:90%


01:08:36.628 --> 01:08:38.214 align:middle line:90%
So what we want is--

01:08:38.214 --> 01:08:51.239 align:middle line:90%


01:08:51.239 --> 01:08:53.489 align:middle line:84%
OK, so we're going to write
a circuit to compute this.

01:08:53.489 --> 01:09:02.590 align:middle line:90%


01:09:02.590 --> 01:09:05.500 align:middle line:84%
Is it OK if I'm very
loose about this?

01:09:05.500 --> 01:09:07.000 align:middle line:84%
I just feel like
all these things

01:09:07.000 --> 01:09:09.680 align:middle line:84%
about exactly how everything
ends up is just terrible.

01:09:09.680 --> 01:09:10.640 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

01:09:10.640 --> 01:09:13.729 align:middle line:90%


01:09:13.729 --> 01:09:15.399 align:middle line:84%
OK, so I guess one
thing you could do

01:09:15.399 --> 01:09:16.840 align:middle line:84%
is you could just
compute and then

01:09:16.840 --> 01:09:19.529 align:middle line:84%
A squared, then A cubed
and A to the fourth.

01:09:19.529 --> 01:09:21.279 align:middle line:84%
But it's not so good
because you can do it

01:09:21.279 --> 01:09:23.810 align:middle line:84%
for T times your
circuit C, depth T.

01:09:23.810 --> 01:09:25.963 align:middle line:90%
So what should you do instead?

01:09:25.963 --> 01:09:27.130 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Repeated squaring.

01:09:27.130 --> 01:09:28.040 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Repeated squaring.

01:09:28.040 --> 01:09:28.180 align:middle line:90%
Great.

01:09:28.180 --> 01:09:28.810 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:09:28.810 --> 01:09:29.310 align:middle line:90%
Yay.

01:09:29.310 --> 01:09:35.390 align:middle line:90%


01:09:35.390 --> 01:09:35.890 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:09:35.890 --> 01:09:39.830 align:middle line:84%
So the idea is we can
compute this matrix A

01:09:39.830 --> 01:09:43.340 align:middle line:84%
So this is going to be very
easy to compute because we

01:09:43.340 --> 01:09:46.412 align:middle line:84%
talked a little bit about how
every entry of this matrix is--

01:09:46.412 --> 01:09:47.870 align:middle line:84%
you can compute it
very easily just

01:09:47.870 --> 01:09:49.550 align:middle line:84%
by looking at the
two configurations

01:09:49.550 --> 01:09:50.800 align:middle line:90%
are that you're interested in.

01:09:50.800 --> 01:09:54.200 align:middle line:90%


01:09:54.200 --> 01:09:57.800 align:middle line:84%
And then you can compute A
squared from this by just--

01:09:57.800 --> 01:10:02.690 align:middle line:84%
I guess you do one division,
one multiplication.

01:10:02.690 --> 01:10:05.600 align:middle line:84%
And then we can do A to the
fourth, A to the eighth and so

01:10:05.600 --> 01:10:06.680 align:middle line:90%
on.

01:10:06.680 --> 01:10:08.670 align:middle line:84%
And eventually we'll
get to A the T,

01:10:08.670 --> 01:10:09.970 align:middle line:90%
which I don't have room for.

01:10:09.970 --> 01:10:19.397 align:middle line:90%


01:10:19.397 --> 01:10:20.730 align:middle line:90%
This is going to be our matrix--

01:10:20.730 --> 01:10:21.365 align:middle line:90%
or our circuit.

01:10:21.365 --> 01:10:24.270 align:middle line:90%


01:10:24.270 --> 01:10:25.620 align:middle line:90%
Let's look at the depth--

01:10:25.620 --> 01:10:28.230 align:middle line:90%
what the depth of the circuit.

01:10:28.230 --> 01:10:31.230 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:10:31.230 --> 01:10:33.817 align:middle line:90%


01:10:33.817 --> 01:10:36.520 align:middle line:90%
OK, so it's, like, log T-ish.

01:10:36.520 --> 01:10:38.038 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But
each, like, thing--

01:10:38.038 --> 01:10:38.830 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

01:10:38.830 --> 01:10:39.330 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:10:39.330 --> 01:10:40.120 align:middle line:90%
Times something.

01:10:40.120 --> 01:10:41.536 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:10:41.536 --> 01:10:43.185 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But don't
you have to do--

01:10:43.185 --> 01:10:46.960 align:middle line:90%


01:10:46.960 --> 01:10:51.500 align:middle line:84%
you have to add up T
thetas to that thing.

01:10:51.500 --> 01:10:53.900 align:middle line:84%
But you can make it a binary
tree, so that will have

01:10:53.900 --> 01:10:54.760 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

01:10:54.760 --> 01:10:55.490 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:10:55.490 --> 01:10:55.990 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:10:55.990 --> 01:10:58.960 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:10:58.960 --> 01:11:00.078 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Poly S.

01:11:00.078 --> 01:11:01.620 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: We're
going to say poly

01:11:01.620 --> 01:11:04.560 align:middle line:84%
S. It's probably, like,
log t times poly S anyway.

01:11:04.560 --> 01:11:08.360 align:middle line:90%


01:11:08.360 --> 01:11:09.400 align:middle line:90%
So that's pretty good.

01:11:09.400 --> 01:11:12.730 align:middle line:84%
Means that it's a verifier
when running this computation

01:11:12.730 --> 01:11:13.870 align:middle line:90%
with the prover in GKR.

01:11:13.870 --> 01:11:15.080 align:middle line:90%
It's a scale to the depths.

01:11:15.080 --> 01:11:18.090 align:middle line:84%
The depth is now in poly S.
What's the size of the circuit?

01:11:18.090 --> 01:11:27.930 align:middle line:90%


01:11:27.930 --> 01:11:33.208 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Poly S-ish,
[INAUDIBLE] 2 to the S.

01:11:33.208 --> 01:11:35.670 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [? Just ?]
[? write ?] [? it out. ?]

01:11:35.670 --> 01:11:38.465 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:11:38.465 --> 01:11:40.712 align:middle line:90%


01:11:40.712 --> 01:11:42.420 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah,
so I guess the matrix

01:11:42.420 --> 01:11:45.135 align:middle line:84%
stuff is going to be size 2
the 2, or something times 2

01:11:45.135 --> 01:11:45.640 align:middle line:90%
to the S.

01:11:45.640 --> 01:11:46.350 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: 2 to the S squared.

01:11:46.350 --> 01:11:47.142 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

01:11:47.142 --> 01:11:51.456 align:middle line:84%
2 to the S. So poly 2 to the S.
2 to the S times 2 to the S is 2

01:11:51.456 --> 01:11:52.110 align:middle line:90%
to the 2S.

01:11:52.110 --> 01:11:52.780 align:middle line:90%
So yeah.

01:11:52.780 --> 01:11:56.530 align:middle line:90%


01:11:56.530 --> 01:11:57.280 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:11:57.280 --> 01:11:58.010 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:11:58.010 --> 01:12:03.200 align:middle line:84%
[LAUGHS] OK, so if we apply
GKR to this, what do we get?

01:12:03.200 --> 01:12:06.176 align:middle line:90%
So let's run GKR.

01:12:06.176 --> 01:12:08.885 align:middle line:84%
Oh, and at the very
top, we need to, then,

01:12:08.885 --> 01:12:11.510 align:middle line:84%
look at the input state and then
check the corresponding entry.

01:12:11.510 --> 01:12:15.050 align:middle line:84%
So you want to read this
particular entry, maybe.

01:12:15.050 --> 01:12:19.120 align:middle line:84%
But you can read it by doing
some of pretty small computation

01:12:19.120 --> 01:12:22.910 align:middle line:84%
on what the input and
accepting computations are.

01:12:22.910 --> 01:12:23.670 align:middle line:90%
So let's run GKR.

01:12:23.670 --> 01:12:26.890 align:middle line:90%


01:12:26.890 --> 01:12:27.915 align:middle line:90%
So prover runtime?

01:12:27.915 --> 01:12:38.727 align:middle line:90%


01:12:38.727 --> 01:12:39.810 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Poly 2 to the S.

01:12:39.810 --> 01:12:40.330 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yay.

01:12:40.330 --> 01:12:41.330 align:middle line:90%
OK I'll let you do this.

01:12:41.330 --> 01:12:44.860 align:middle line:90%


01:12:44.860 --> 01:12:45.585 align:middle line:90%
Verifier runtime?

01:12:45.585 --> 01:12:50.000 align:middle line:90%


01:12:50.000 --> 01:12:50.910 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Poly S.

01:12:50.910 --> 01:12:51.660 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yay.

01:12:51.660 --> 01:12:55.100 align:middle line:84%
OK I guess that's what
we wanted to prove, huh?

01:12:55.100 --> 01:12:56.160 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:12:56.160 --> 01:12:56.660 align:middle line:90%
Nice.

01:12:56.660 --> 01:12:57.830 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:12:57.830 --> 01:12:59.290 align:middle line:90%
Good work.

01:12:59.290 --> 01:13:00.870 align:middle line:84%
Are there any
questions about this?

01:13:00.870 --> 01:13:01.950 align:middle line:90%
Or is this-- does it make sense?

01:13:01.950 --> 01:13:02.090 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:13:02.090 --> 01:13:04.423 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Sorry, I might have
missed an important point,

01:13:04.423 --> 01:13:07.045 align:middle line:84%
but where is the cost of
actually doing the squaring?

01:13:07.045 --> 01:13:08.907 align:middle line:90%


01:13:08.907 --> 01:13:11.240 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: I guess a really
good depth in the circuit

01:13:11.240 --> 01:13:13.430 align:middle line:84%
is each square you do is
going to take some more

01:13:13.430 --> 01:13:14.460 align:middle line:90%
depth of your circuit.

01:13:14.460 --> 01:13:14.755 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:13:14.755 --> 01:13:16.463 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Is that
what you're saying?

01:13:16.463 --> 01:13:18.440 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah,
that's the question.

01:13:18.440 --> 01:13:20.010 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Maybe I didn't
understand the question.

01:13:20.010 --> 01:13:21.000 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Oh, is it--

01:13:21.000 --> 01:13:23.350 align:middle line:90%
what is the cost of squaring A?

01:13:23.350 --> 01:13:25.350 align:middle line:84%
Just going from A to A
squared, what's the cost?

01:13:25.350 --> 01:13:25.610 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Sure.

01:13:25.610 --> 01:13:26.110 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:13:26.110 --> 01:13:29.630 align:middle line:90%
So how do you compute a squared.

01:13:29.630 --> 01:13:31.550 align:middle line:84%
You can do every row
and every column.

01:13:31.550 --> 01:13:34.133 align:middle line:90%
And then what if I add them?

01:13:34.133 --> 01:13:34.675 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Oh.

01:13:34.675 --> 01:13:35.930 align:middle line:90%
So is it-- you're just doing--

01:13:35.930 --> 01:13:38.055 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: You're just
doing matrix application.

01:13:38.055 --> 01:13:38.768 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I see.

01:13:38.768 --> 01:13:41.060 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: So maybe you
can tell me how [INAUDIBLE].

01:13:41.060 --> 01:13:43.328 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:13:43.328 --> 01:13:43.828 align:middle line:90%


01:13:43.828 --> 01:13:46.290 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:13:46.290 --> 01:13:48.340 align:middle line:90%


01:13:48.340 --> 01:13:50.987 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But are we supposed
to know how that thing that you

01:13:50.987 --> 01:13:53.320 align:middle line:84%
said we need to prove to you--
are we supposed to know--

01:13:53.320 --> 01:13:55.320 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Oh, yeah,
that's a great question.

01:13:55.320 --> 01:14:00.040 align:middle line:84%
So earlier-- I already
erased it, I think.

01:14:00.040 --> 01:14:00.540 align:middle line:90%
Wait.

01:14:00.540 --> 01:14:03.082 align:middle line:84%
Are there any more questions
about this before we go to that?

01:14:03.082 --> 01:14:05.507 align:middle line:90%


01:14:05.507 --> 01:14:07.520 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So we're asking
you this to prove that

01:14:07.520 --> 01:14:08.930 align:middle line:90%
because [INAUDIBLE].

01:14:08.930 --> 01:14:12.628 align:middle line:90%


01:14:12.628 --> 01:14:13.920 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: You can't use IP.

01:14:13.920 --> 01:14:17.520 align:middle line:90%


01:14:17.520 --> 01:14:18.990 align:middle line:90%
Just use the logic you just had.

01:14:18.990 --> 01:14:20.030 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK, OK.

01:14:20.030 --> 01:14:20.822 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

01:14:20.822 --> 01:14:24.690 align:middle line:84%
So how do we construct
the circuit now?

01:14:24.690 --> 01:14:26.310 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:14:26.310 --> 01:14:39.183 align:middle line:90%


01:14:39.183 --> 01:14:40.600 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So that's
another reason

01:14:40.600 --> 01:14:42.444 align:middle line:84%
we need logspace
uniform [INAUDIBLE].

01:14:42.444 --> 01:14:43.694 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It runs in logspace.

01:14:43.694 --> 01:14:46.030 align:middle line:90%
That's a Turing machine.

01:14:46.030 --> 01:14:47.425 align:middle line:90%
So [INAUDIBLE]

01:14:47.425 --> 01:14:48.050 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:14:48.050 --> 01:14:52.280 align:middle line:90%


01:14:52.280 --> 01:14:53.250 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, OK.

01:14:53.250 --> 01:14:55.820 align:middle line:84%
So this is basically a
scaled-down version of that.

01:14:55.820 --> 01:14:57.900 align:middle line:90%
This is logspace uniform.

01:14:57.900 --> 01:14:59.180 align:middle line:90%
So you're running in logspace.

01:14:59.180 --> 01:15:01.190 align:middle line:84%
Then you're running
in time space S.

01:15:01.190 --> 01:15:04.243 align:middle line:84%
So this is a exponentially
smaller version of that.

01:15:04.243 --> 01:15:05.660 align:middle line:84%
So all the parameters
over there--

01:15:05.660 --> 01:15:06.810 align:middle line:90%
you scale them down.

01:15:06.810 --> 01:15:08.360 align:middle line:90%
So we have the depth over here.

01:15:08.360 --> 01:15:14.150 align:middle line:84%
Was poly S. The log of that
is log S. The log of the size

01:15:14.150 --> 01:15:16.700 align:middle line:84%
here is S So we have all
those things we wanted.

01:15:16.700 --> 01:15:22.030 align:middle line:90%


01:15:22.030 --> 01:15:26.030 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How do you incorporate
that input into this--

01:15:26.030 --> 01:15:27.888 align:middle line:84%
you need to know what
the starting state is.

01:15:27.888 --> 01:15:29.888 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: With this
one or with [INAUDIBLE]?

01:15:29.888 --> 01:15:31.330 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: With that one.

01:15:31.330 --> 01:15:32.580 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: The input state.

01:15:32.580 --> 01:15:33.890 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:15:33.890 --> 01:15:36.230 align:middle line:84%
So I think the way I
normally think-- or I

01:15:36.230 --> 01:15:38.895 align:middle line:84%
like to think about it is
you only check the input

01:15:38.895 --> 01:15:40.520 align:middle line:84%
configuration at the
very end, once you

01:15:40.520 --> 01:15:42.105 align:middle line:90%
complete the entire thing.

01:15:42.105 --> 01:15:44.480 align:middle line:84%
So then you only have to read
the input, comma, accepting

01:15:44.480 --> 01:15:46.160 align:middle line:90%
configurations entry.

01:15:46.160 --> 01:15:49.250 align:middle line:84%
And there you can
just do a formula

01:15:49.250 --> 01:15:51.350 align:middle line:84%
to read off what it
says and then look

01:15:51.350 --> 01:15:53.990 align:middle line:90%
at the corresponding value.

01:15:53.990 --> 01:15:56.870 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So almost the
entire circuit is just--

01:15:56.870 --> 01:15:58.100 align:middle line:90%
doesn't even read the input.

01:15:58.100 --> 01:15:59.892 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah,
it's only the very end.

01:15:59.892 --> 01:16:08.890 align:middle line:90%


01:16:08.890 --> 01:16:11.110 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But, I mean,
this is very accepting,

01:16:11.110 --> 01:16:14.670 align:middle line:84%
and that one doesn't
really accept it in print.

01:16:14.670 --> 01:16:16.446 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

01:16:16.446 --> 01:16:18.008 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:16:18.008 --> 01:16:19.050 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah, yeah.

01:16:19.050 --> 01:16:19.550 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:16:19.550 --> 01:16:25.580 align:middle line:84%
So maybe-- yeah, so I guess
each configuration corresponds

01:16:25.580 --> 01:16:27.450 align:middle line:90%
to-- it's a configuration.

01:16:27.450 --> 01:16:30.170 align:middle line:84%
So there's some data about
what the gate's output--

01:16:30.170 --> 01:16:32.040 align:middle line:90%
gate value is or something.

01:16:32.040 --> 01:16:34.570 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you can read
off the value there.

01:16:34.570 --> 01:16:38.930 align:middle line:90%


01:16:38.930 --> 01:16:41.030 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Each entry,
[INAUDIBLE] [? comma-- ?] is

01:16:41.030 --> 01:16:44.366 align:middle line:84%
the gate here [? or not, ?] and
then each entry [INAUDIBLE]?

01:16:44.366 --> 01:16:48.760 align:middle line:90%


01:16:48.760 --> 01:16:51.470 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And this sounds like
an easy add, mult situation,

01:16:51.470 --> 01:16:51.970 align:middle line:90%
right?

01:16:51.970 --> 01:16:53.798 align:middle line:84%
Because [? it's just ?]
[? computation? ?]

01:16:53.798 --> 01:16:54.590 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

01:16:54.590 --> 01:16:57.550 align:middle line:84%
The important point here is
that computing the matrix A

01:16:57.550 --> 01:16:58.550 align:middle line:90%
is very easy.

01:16:58.550 --> 01:17:01.210 align:middle line:84%
That's, really,
the key thing here.

01:17:01.210 --> 01:17:03.850 align:middle line:84%
And that's also why,
when we said what--

01:17:03.850 --> 01:17:04.970 align:middle line:90%
we were behind.

01:17:04.970 --> 01:17:07.940 align:middle line:84%
But when we said
what A looks like,

01:17:07.940 --> 01:17:10.958 align:middle line:84%
it really had to be also
where the Turing pointer was,

01:17:10.958 --> 01:17:13.000 align:middle line:84%
because if you didn't know
where the pointer was,

01:17:13.000 --> 01:17:14.300 align:middle line:90%
it could be very large.

01:17:14.300 --> 01:17:16.300 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:17:16.300 --> 01:17:24.360 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:17:24.360 --> 01:17:29.040 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So you
can factor out what

01:17:29.040 --> 01:17:31.858 align:middle line:84%
you're saying into the form
of-- if you have a low space

01:17:31.858 --> 01:17:33.900 align:middle line:84%
computation, you can turn
to a low-depth circuit.

01:17:33.900 --> 01:17:37.063 align:middle line:84%
Is that a is that a fair
[INAUDIBLE]? that you've used?

01:17:37.063 --> 01:17:38.730 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah,
it's actually a kind

01:17:38.730 --> 01:17:40.658 align:middle line:90%
of interesting statement, huh?

01:17:40.658 --> 01:17:41.200 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

01:17:41.200 --> 01:17:43.658 align:middle line:84%
I didn't know that you could
do this until recently either.

01:17:43.658 --> 01:17:47.270 align:middle line:90%


01:17:47.270 --> 01:17:48.950 align:middle line:84%
So I guess-- the
interesting thing here

01:17:48.950 --> 01:17:52.200 align:middle line:84%
is instead of being a really
tall and skinny computation,

01:17:52.200 --> 01:17:55.490 align:middle line:84%
you can turn it into a circuit
that's really fat and short.

01:17:55.490 --> 01:17:57.920 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: That's why we're
getting [INAUDIBLE] depth, not

01:17:57.920 --> 01:17:58.835 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

01:17:58.835 --> 01:18:04.365 align:middle line:90%


01:18:04.365 --> 01:18:04.990 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Wait.

01:18:04.990 --> 01:18:05.320 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

01:18:05.320 --> 01:18:05.820 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:18:05.820 --> 01:18:08.240 align:middle line:84%
I was just-- in terms of
where we're at in class.

01:18:08.240 --> 01:18:10.390 align:middle line:90%
So we just proved this theorem.

01:18:10.390 --> 01:18:14.770 align:middle line:84%
And is this the same reasoning
for why the logic conformity

01:18:14.770 --> 01:18:16.310 align:middle line:90%
thing works out, too--

01:18:16.310 --> 01:18:17.380 align:middle line:90%
by the same [INAUDIBLE]?

01:18:17.380 --> 01:18:18.463 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: You tell me.

01:18:18.463 --> 01:18:18.970 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:18:18.970 --> 01:18:20.980 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:18:20.980 --> 01:18:22.780 align:middle line:90%
You tell me.

01:18:22.780 --> 01:18:23.680 align:middle line:90%
I'm delegating.

01:18:23.680 --> 01:18:25.650 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:18:25.650 --> 01:18:27.942 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's kind of weird
because you don't actually

01:18:27.942 --> 01:18:29.030 align:middle line:90%
take A as input.

01:18:29.030 --> 01:18:32.290 align:middle line:84%
A is [? hardcoded ?]
into the circuit, right?

01:18:32.290 --> 01:18:34.520 align:middle line:84%
That's why the input can
still be the small thing.

01:18:34.520 --> 01:18:37.045 align:middle line:84%
That's just describing
the starting state.

01:18:37.045 --> 01:18:39.070 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah,
The Turing machine, M,

01:18:39.070 --> 01:18:41.330 align:middle line:84%
is what determines what the
circuit action looks like.

01:18:41.330 --> 01:18:42.830 align:middle line:84%
So maybe as part of
the input of this,

01:18:42.830 --> 01:18:44.500 align:middle line:84%
you're also going to have a
description-- like, the Turing

01:18:44.500 --> 01:18:45.723 align:middle line:90%
machine in its entirety.

01:18:45.723 --> 01:18:48.140 align:middle line:84%
The Turing machines are finite
size, so it doesn't matter.

01:18:48.140 --> 01:18:48.765 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:18:48.765 --> 01:18:51.015 align:middle line:90%


01:18:51.015 --> 01:18:52.390 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And
that's why we don't

01:18:52.390 --> 01:18:55.180 align:middle line:84%
need to worry about complicated
add, mult predicates inside,

01:18:55.180 --> 01:18:56.972 align:middle line:84%
because it's actually
just looks like this.

01:18:56.972 --> 01:19:07.225 align:middle line:90%


01:19:07.225 --> 01:19:08.850 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I'm still
kind of weirded out

01:19:08.850 --> 01:19:12.660 align:middle line:90%
by the fact that all of that--

01:19:12.660 --> 01:19:14.850 align:middle line:84%
until the star at
the top, all of this

01:19:14.850 --> 01:19:16.620 align:middle line:84%
is not even dependent
on the input.

01:19:16.620 --> 01:19:20.240 align:middle line:84%
So it's like-- in this case,
it's just going to be constant.

01:19:20.240 --> 01:19:21.330 align:middle line:90%
Like, [INAUDIBLE].

01:19:21.330 --> 01:19:23.970 align:middle line:84%
So then why are we going
through the trouble

01:19:23.970 --> 01:19:26.700 align:middle line:84%
of computing all these
layers, instead of just

01:19:26.700 --> 01:19:28.440 align:middle line:90%
using the top layer, where we--

01:19:28.440 --> 01:19:32.310 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: I mean, how
do you hold the top layer?

01:19:32.310 --> 01:19:34.170 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So you're
saying that it's easier

01:19:34.170 --> 01:19:37.812 align:middle line:84%
to encode the entire computation
thing than just the result.

01:19:37.812 --> 01:19:39.270 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: It's
easy to say what

01:19:39.270 --> 01:19:43.130 align:middle line:84%
you have to do to compute it
than to actually compute it.

01:19:43.130 --> 01:19:47.238 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:19:47.238 --> 01:19:52.875 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] M,
not C. M [INAUDIBLE] C.

01:19:52.875 --> 01:19:55.125 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Or brackets used
a compressed representation

01:19:55.125 --> 01:19:59.727 align:middle line:90%
of C. [INAUDIBLE]

01:19:59.727 --> 01:20:02.060 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But I'm saying, why
don't you just hardcode it

01:20:02.060 --> 01:20:04.150 align:middle line:90%
in the top layer of M?

01:20:04.150 --> 01:20:08.540 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: That
would [INAUDIBLE] C.

01:20:08.540 --> 01:20:10.925 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: They
didn't even [INAUDIBLE]

01:20:10.925 --> 01:20:12.050 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Well, it's just--

01:20:12.050 --> 01:20:14.860 align:middle line:84%
I mean, it's just a
string of size of C.

01:20:14.860 --> 01:20:19.006 align:middle line:84%
So you can't hardcode
[INAUDIBLE] solution.

01:20:19.006 --> 01:20:19.548 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK.

01:20:19.548 --> 01:20:22.486 align:middle line:84%
It's weird, but I
can kind of see.

01:20:22.486 --> 01:20:24.718 align:middle line:90%


01:20:24.718 --> 01:20:26.885 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So basically, we
went through this trouble

01:20:26.885 --> 01:20:28.677 align:middle line:84%
and we were able to go
through this trouble

01:20:28.677 --> 01:20:31.320 align:middle line:84%
because we assume that C
can be described succinctly,

01:20:31.320 --> 01:20:32.790 align:middle line:90%
like logspace succinctly.

01:20:32.790 --> 01:20:36.558 align:middle line:90%


01:20:36.558 --> 01:20:37.350 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

01:20:37.350 --> 01:20:40.822 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is poly log
S uniform [INAUDIBLE]?

01:20:40.822 --> 01:20:42.697 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: I'm going
to delegate-- push it

01:20:42.697 --> 01:20:44.420 align:middle line:90%
to somebody else.

01:20:44.420 --> 01:20:48.380 align:middle line:90%
I don't know how log blows up.

01:20:48.380 --> 01:20:51.140 align:middle line:84%
Probably not,
because if you take--

01:20:51.140 --> 01:20:52.880 align:middle line:84%
the transition
matrix A you have is

01:20:52.880 --> 01:20:55.110 align:middle line:84%
going to be 2 to the
description size or whatever.

01:20:55.110 --> 01:20:56.430 align:middle line:90%
Like, the space.

01:20:56.430 --> 01:20:58.610 align:middle line:84%
So if you have larger
space, it's really bad.

01:20:58.610 --> 01:21:01.760 align:middle line:90%


01:21:01.760 --> 01:21:03.780 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But
space S gets turned

01:21:03.780 --> 01:21:07.740 align:middle line:84%
into depth like S squared,
roughly, in this transformation.

01:21:07.740 --> 01:21:08.580 align:middle line:90%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah.

01:21:08.580 --> 01:21:11.824 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So if we have
space S squared to start,

01:21:11.824 --> 01:21:15.160 align:middle line:90%
we can definitely [INAUDIBLE].

01:21:15.160 --> 01:21:16.660 align:middle line:84%
RACHEL ZHANG: Yeah,
but the question

01:21:16.660 --> 01:21:19.650 align:middle line:90%
is the prover runtime, right?

01:21:19.650 --> 01:21:21.400 align:middle line:84%
But the reason why
we're doing any of this

01:21:21.400 --> 01:21:22.410 align:middle line:90%
is because the prover runtime.

01:21:22.410 --> 01:21:24.577 align:middle line:84%
You actually get an advantage
in the prover runtime.

01:21:24.577 --> 01:21:35.220 align:middle line:90%


01:21:35.220 --> 01:21:36.560 align:middle line:90%
So we're done early.

01:21:36.560 --> 01:21:39.080 align:middle line:84%
So maybe we can all
